QB.add(1,[{"id":1001,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","f":"ns05","s":"A neuroanatomy demonstrator holds up a whole brain and asks a student to point to its <b>dorsal</b> surface, then its <b>rostral</b> pole. Using the terms as they apply to the brain (not the rest of the body), which pair of locations should the student indicate?","o":["The top of the brain, then the front of the brain","The back of the brain, then the front of the brain","The top of the brain, then the back of the brain","The underside of the brain, then the front of the brain","The back of the brain, then the top of the brain"],"e":"In the brain the directional terms differ from the rest of the body. <b>Dorsal = top</b>, <b>ventral = underside</b>, <b>rostral = front</b> and <b>caudal = back</b>. Picture the brain sitting on the spine of a four-legged animal: the top is dorsal and the nose end is rostral.","w":["“Back” is what dorsal means in the rest of the body, not in the brain.","The back of the brain is caudal, not rostral.","The underside of the brain is ventral.","This mixes the body meaning of dorsal (back) with an invented meaning of rostral."],"k":"In the brain: dorsal = top, ventral = underside, rostral = front, caudal = back.","rn":[["","How do you remember the brain directions?","How do you remember the brain directions?","Imagine the brain on top of a four-legged animal's spine; Dorsal = top; Ventral = underside; Rostral = nose end; Caudal = tail end"]]},{"id":1002,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","s":"In the rest of the body, the term <b>ventral</b> describes the front. Which term describes the <b>front</b> of the brain?","o":["Rostral","Ventral","Caudal","Dorsal","Superior"],"e":"The front of the brain is <b>rostral</b>. Ventral (front in the body) becomes the <b>underside</b> in the brain, which is why the lecturer covered these terms first.","w":["In the brain, ventral means the underside.","Caudal is the back of the brain.","Dorsal is the top of the brain.","Superior means “up” in the rest of the body; it is not the brain term for front."],"k":"Body ventral (front) ≠ brain ventral (underside); the brain's front is rostral.","rn":[["","In the brain, what does rostral mean?","In the brain, what does rostral mean?","Front"]]},{"id":1003,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","s":"A radiologist describes a lesion as lying on the <b>caudal, ventral</b> aspect of a brain structure. Where is the lesion?","o":["At the back and on the underside of the structure","At the front and on the underside of the structure","At the back and on the top of the structure","At the front and on the top of the structure","Towards the midline and on the underside of the structure"],"e":"<b>Caudal</b> = back and <b>ventral</b> = underside when the terms are applied to the brain. Knowing the directions lets you orientate yourself to any part of the brain.","w":["Front would be rostral.","Top would be dorsal.","Front and top would be rostral and dorsal.","Towards the midline is medial; caudal refers to the back."],"k":"Combine brain terms: caudal = back, ventral = underside."},{"id":1004,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","f":"ns05","s":"On a coronal slice of the brain, a structure lying close to the midline is compared with one near the outer edge of the hemisphere. Which terms correctly describe them?","o":["Medial (near the midline) and lateral (near the outer edge)","Lateral (near the midline) and medial (near the outer edge)","Rostral (near the midline) and caudal (near the outer edge)","Dorsal (near the midline) and ventral (near the outer edge)","Proximal (near the midline) and distal (near the outer edge)"],"e":"In the brain, <b>medial and lateral keep their usual meanings</b>: medial = innermost (towards the midline) and lateral = outermost (away from the midline).","w":["This is reversed.","Rostral and caudal describe front and back, not distance from the midline.","Dorsal and ventral describe top and underside in the brain.","These are not the terms used for midline position in the lecture."],"k":"Medial = towards the midline; lateral = away from the midline (same in brain and body).","rn":[["","What is Medial?","What is Medial?","Towards the midline; Innermost"],["","What is Lateral?","What is Lateral?","Away from the midline; Outermost"]]},{"id":1005,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","s":"A student reading a textbook finds a brain section described as <b>axial</b>. Which plane is this the same as?","o":["Horizontal","Coronal","Sagittal","Midsagittal","Frontal-oblique"],"e":"Terminology is interchangeable between textbooks: the <b>horizontal</b> plane is also called <b>axial or transverse</b>. You will meet the alternative names in many places.","w":["Coronal is the vertical “crown” cut from ear to ear.","Sagittal separates left from right.","A midsagittal cut is still a sagittal cut.","This is not a plane named in the lecture."],"k":"Horizontal = axial = transverse.","rn":[["","What is Horizontal Plane?","What is Horizontal Plane?","Parallel to the ground; Also called axial / transverse"]]},{"id":1006,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","f":"ns07","s":"A brain is sliced with a vertical cut that runs from one ear to the other, in the position a crown would sit. Which plane is this?","o":["Coronal","Sagittal","Horizontal","Transverse","Axial"],"e":"The lecturer's tip: <b>coronal = like a crown</b>, a vertical cut from ear to ear. Sagittal splits left from right, and horizontal (axial/transverse) is parallel to the ground.","w":["A sagittal cut separates the left and right sides.","A horizontal cut is parallel to the ground.","Transverse is another name for horizontal.","Axial is another name for horizontal."],"k":"Coronal = crown, ear to ear.","rn":[["","What is Coronal Plane?","What is Coronal Plane?","Vertical cut from ear to ear; Like a crown"]]},{"id":1007,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","f":"ns07","s":"Two students section the same brain, one coronally and one sagittally, and are surprised that their slices look completely different. What principle explains this?","o":["Cutting one structure in different planes gives different forms","The left and right hemispheres have different internal structures","Coronal sections show only white matter, sagittal only grey","Sagittal sections remove the grey matter","Brain tissue changes shape after fixation"],"e":"The slide states that <b>cutting the same structure in different ways results in various forms</b>: the same brain looks completely different in each plane. This is why knowing the planes matters for reading sections and scans.","w":["The lecture did not explain the differences by hemisphere.","Both planes show grey and white matter; the forms differ with the plane.","Sectioning does not remove grey matter.","Fixation was not discussed."],"k":"The same structure looks different in each plane.","rn":[["","What happens when you cut the same structure in different planes?","What happens when you cut the same structure in different planes?","It results in various forms; The brain looks completely different in each plane"]]},{"id":1008,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","f":"ns06","s":"A research group produces a coloured image showing the pathways that run through the brain and link its regions. Which type of descriptive anatomy does this illustrate?","o":["Connectional anatomy","Surface anatomy","Sectional anatomy","Lobular anatomy","Functional specificity"],"e":"<b>Connectional anatomy</b> describes the pathways (<b>tracts</b>) running through the brain. We never see these structures directly, but such images show how they are organised.","w":["Surface anatomy is the outside of the brain and its landmarks (gyri and sulci).","Sectional anatomy is the brain cut into slices, as in an MRI scan.","Lobular organisation divides the brain into lobes; it is not one of the three types of descriptive anatomy.","Functional specificity is about different lobes having different functions."],"k":"Surface, sectional and connectional anatomy; connectional = tracts.","rn":[["","Connectional Anatomy","Connectional Anatomy","The pathways (tracts) through the brain; Connecting regions"],["","What are the 3 types of descriptive anatomy?","What are the 3 types of descriptive anatomy?","Surface anatomy; Sectional anatomy; Connectional anatomy"]]},{"id":1009,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","s":"A student studying an MRI scan is looking at the brain as a series of slices. Which type of descriptive anatomy is this?","o":["Sectional anatomy","Surface anatomy","Connectional anatomy","Regional anatomy","Comparative anatomy"],"e":"<b>Sectional anatomy</b> is the brain cut into slices, as seen in an MRI scan.","w":["Surface anatomy is the outside of the brain.","Connectional anatomy shows the tracts.","This is not one of the three types in the lecture.","This is not one of the three types in the lecture."],"k":"Sectional anatomy = slices (e.g. MRI).","rn":[["","Sectional Anatomy","Sectional Anatomy","The brain cut into slices; e.g. an MRI scan"]]},{"id":1010,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Why is the surface of the cerebral cortex covered in gyri and sulci rather than being smooth?","o":["To maximise the surface area of the cortex","To allow cerebrospinal fluid to drain","To separate grey matter from white matter","To divide the brain into forebrain, midbrain and hindbrain","To anchor the brain to the skull"],"e":"The uneven surface of <b>gyri (ridges/elevations)</b> and <b>sulci (depressions)</b> <b>maximises the surface area</b> of the cerebral cortex. The cortex is highly folded to fit into the skull, like a crumpled sheet of paper.","w":["Not covered in the lecture as the reason for folding.","Folding does not separate grey from white matter.","Those divisions are not made by gyri and sulci.","Not given as a reason in the lecture."],"k":"Gyri and sulci maximise cortical surface area.","rn":[["","What do gyri and sulci do?","What do gyri and sulci do?","Make the surface uneven; Maximise surface area for the cerebral cortex"]]},{"id":1011,"t":"Divisions of the Nervous System","s":"Which of the following lists contains <b>only</b> components of the peripheral nervous system?","o":["Cranial nerves, spinal nerves, sensory receptors","Brain, cranial nerves, spinal nerves","Spinal cord, spinal nerves, sensory receptors","Brain, spinal cord, sensory receptors","Spinal cord, cranial nerves, sensory organs"],"e":"The PNS comprises <b>cranial nerves (12 pairs)</b>, <b>spinal nerves (31 pairs)</b> and <b>sensory organs/receptors</b>. The CNS is the <b>brain and spinal cord</b>.","w":["The brain is CNS.","The spinal cord is CNS.","Brain and spinal cord are both CNS.","The spinal cord is CNS."],"k":"CNS = brain + spinal cord; PNS = cranial nerves, spinal nerves, sensory organs/receptors.","rn":[["","What are the components of the PNS?","What are the components of the PNS?","Cranial nerves; Spinal nerves; Sensory organs / receptors"]]},{"id":1012,"t":"Divisions of the Nervous System","s":"How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the <b>thoracic</b> region, and how many pairs of spinal nerves are there in total?","o":["12 thoracic; 31 in total","12 thoracic; 33 in total","8 thoracic; 31 in total","5 thoracic; 31 in total","12 thoracic; 24 in total"],"e":"There are <b>31 pairs</b> of spinal nerves: <b>8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal</b>. Memory aid: breakfast at 8, lunch at 12, dinner at 5, supper at 5, bed at 1.","w":["33 is not the number of spinal nerve pairs.","8 is the number of cervical pairs.","5 is the number of lumbar (or sacral) pairs.","24 is not the total."],"k":"31 pairs: 8C, 12T, 5L, 5S, 1Co.","rn":[["","How are the 31 pairs of spinal nerves divided?","How are the 31 pairs of spinal nerves divided?","8 cervical; 12 thoracic; 5 lumbar; 5 sacral; 1 coccygeal; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1013,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A student adds the cervical, lumbar and coccygeal spinal nerve pairs together. What total do they get?","o":["14","13","19","18","26"],"e":"Cervical <b>8</b> + lumbar <b>5</b> + coccygeal <b>1</b> = <b>14</b>. The full breakdown is 8C, 12T, 5L, 5S, 1Co = 31 pairs.","w":["This would be 8 + 5 with the coccygeal pair left out.","This also adds the 5 sacral pairs (8 + 5 + 5 + 1).","This mistakes the coccygeal count for 5.","This adds the thoracic pairs instead of the coccygeal."],"k":"Know each number: 8C, 12T, 5L, 5S, 1Co.","rn":[["","What is proprioception?","What is proprioception?","Knowing where your body parts are; e.g. knowing where your feet are without looking"],["","How are the cranial nerves numbered?","How are the cranial nerves numbered?","Roman numerals; I to XII"]]},{"id":1014,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Although classed as part of the PNS, two cranial nerves might more accurately be considered part of the CNS on a <b>structural</b> level. Which are they?","o":["Olfactory (I) and optic (II)","Optic (II) and oculomotor (III)","Olfactory (I) and vagus (X)","Trigeminal (V) and facial (VII)","Vagus (X) and accessory (XI)"],"e":"The slide footnote states that although the <b>olfactory (CN I)</b> and <b>optic (CN II)</b> nerves are considered PNS, it may be more accurate structurally to consider them part of the CNS.","w":["Only CN I and II were named.","Only CN I and II were named.","Only CN I and II were named.","Only CN I and II were named."],"k":"CN I and CN II are structurally more like CNS.","rn":[["","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","Primary auditory cortex; Primary olfactory (smell) cortex; Learning and memory"],["","How do you remember the lobe functions?","How do you remember the lobe functions?","Frontal = Function (executive) + movement; Parietal = Pain / touch; Temporal = Tunes (hearing) + memory; Occipital = Optics (vision)"]]},{"id":1015,"t":"Divisions of the Nervous System","s":"Why are cranial nerves always written as Roman numerals (I–XII)?","o":["It is an academic convention dating back to the second century","It shows the order in which the nerves develop in the embryo","It distinguishes the motor cranial nerves from the sensory ones","It shows the vertebral level at which each nerve leaves the skull","It mirrors the region-letter naming used for the spinal nerves"],"e":"The lecturer explained that Roman numerals are the <b>academic convention</b>, which can be traced back to the <b>second century</b>. Spinal nerves, by contrast, are named by region letter and number.","w":["The lecturer gave no developmental reason; it is a naming convention.","Roman numerals do not code for motor versus sensory function.","Cranial nerves arise from the brain/brainstem, not at vertebral levels.","Spinal nerves use region letter + number (e.g. T12); cranial nerves use Roman numerals, a different convention."],"k":"Cranial nerves I–XII in Roman numerals by academic convention."},{"id":1016,"t":"Divisions of the Nervous System","s":"Which protective structures surround the brain and the spinal cord respectively?","o":["The skull and the vertebral column","The vertebral column and the skull","The skull and the sternum","The meninges only, for both","The blood–brain barrier and the vertebral canal"],"e":"The brain (about <b>1,200 g</b> in an adult) is protected by the <b>skull</b>, and the spinal cord by the <b>vertebral column</b> (it lies in the vertebral canal).","w":["Reversed.","The sternum does not protect the spinal cord.","The lecture named the skull and vertebral column.","The BBB is a selectively permeable barrier around vessels, not bony protection."],"k":"Brain: skull. Spinal cord: vertebral column.","rn":[["","What protects the spinal cord?","What protects the spinal cord?","The vertebral column"]]},{"id":1017,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","f":"ns09","s":"Early anatomists argued that the nervous system was a single continuous network. Which statement best describes the view that replaced this?","o":["Discrete individual cells that are mutually dependent on one another","Discrete individual cells that each work independently of the others","A continuous web of neurons held together by glial cells","Neurons fused at their synapses into a single syncytium","Discrete cells in the PNS but one continuous network in the CNS"],"e":"The <b>neuronal doctrine</b>, associated with <b>Santiago Ramón y Cajal</b>, states that the nervous system is made of <b>discrete, individual cells which are mutually dependent</b>. It replaced the now-obsolete <b>reticular theory</b> (one continuous network).","w":["The neuronal doctrine says the cells are discrete but mutually dependent, not independent.","This is essentially the reticular view (one continuous network), which was replaced.","Fusion into one network is the reticular theory, now obsolete.","The doctrine applies to the whole nervous system, not just the PNS."],"k":"Neuronal doctrine (Cajal) replaced reticular theory.","rn":[["","What is Neuronal Doctrine?","What is Neuronal Doctrine?","Nervous system is made of discrete, individual cells; Which are mutually dependent on one another"]]},{"id":1018,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","f":"ns09","s":"Santiago Ramón y Cajal is described as “the father of neuroscience”. Which statement about him is correct?","o":["He lived 1852–1934 and gave us the neuronal doctrine","He lived 1852–1934 and proposed the reticular theory","He first described the oligodendrocyte and named it","He showed that adult neurons can proliferate after injury","He described the blood–brain barrier formed by astrocytes"],"e":"<b>Cajal (1852–1934)</b> gave us the <b>neuronal doctrine</b>. His famous hand-drawn images of neurons appear on the title slide, made by looking at human neurons down a microscope.","w":["The reticular theory is the view Cajal’s neuronal doctrine replaced.","The oligodendrocyte was first described by Pío del Río Hortega.","Neurons are post-mitotic; Cajal is credited with the neuronal doctrine.","The BBB was not attributed to Cajal in the lecture."],"k":"Cajal (1852–1934): neuronal doctrine.","rn":[["","What did Cajal give us?","What did Cajal give us?","The neuronal doctrine; Which replaced the reticular theory"]]},{"id":1019,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which labelling of regions A–D is correct?","o":["A frontal; B parietal; C occipital; D temporal","A frontal; B temporal; C occipital; D parietal","A parietal; B frontal; C occipital; D temporal","A frontal; B occipital; C parietal; D temporal","A frontal; B parietal; C temporal; D occipital"],"e":"Frontal (A): primary motor cortex, executive functions, thinking and planning. Parietal (B): primary somatosensory cortex (pain, touch, proprioception). Occipital (C): primary visual cortex. Temporal (D): primary auditory and olfactory cortex, learning and memory. E is the cerebellum.","w":["D lies below the lateral fissure: temporal; B lies behind the central sulcus: parietal.","A is in front of the central sulcus: frontal.","C is at the very back: occipital.","C is the occipital lobe; D the temporal lobe."],"k":"Front to back: frontal, parietal, occipital; below: temporal.","img":{"src":"1/lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 lobes of the brain?","What are the 4 lobes of the brain?","Frontal; Parietal; Temporal; Occipital"],["","How do you remember the lobe functions?","How do you remember the lobe functions?","Frontal = Function (executive) + movement; Parietal = Pain / touch; Temporal = Tunes (hearing) + memory; Occipital = Optics (vision)"]]},{"id":1020,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Approximately how many neurons and how many synapses are there in the human nervous system?","o":["8.6 × 10¹⁰ neurons and 10¹⁴ synapses","8.6 × 10¹⁴ neurons and 10¹⁰ synapses","10–20 × 10⁹ neurons and 10¹⁴ synapses","8.6 × 10¹⁰ neurons and 10¹⁰ synapses","10¹⁴ neurons and 8.6 × 10¹⁰ synapses"],"e":"About <b>8.6 × 10¹⁰ (86 billion) neurons</b> and <b>10¹⁴ synapses</b> interconnecting them. The lecturer stressed the principle over the exact figure, which varies between sources.","w":["The exponents are swapped.","10–20 billion is the number of neurons in the cerebral cortex.","There are far more synapses than neurons.","Swapped."],"k":"~86 billion neurons; 10¹⁴ synapses.","rn":[["","How many neurons are in the nervous system?","How many neurons are in the nervous system?","About 8.6 x 10^10; 86,000,000,000 (86 billion)"]]},{"id":1021,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A research team reduces an MRI scan to one cubic millimetre (a single voxel) of cortex. Roughly how many neurons would this contain?","o":["About 100,000, depending on the density of the region","About 10,000–12,000, depending on the region","About 1,000,000, the same in every region","About 100,000, the same in every region","About 1,000, depending on the density of the region"],"e":"The lecturer said one cubic millimetre (one voxel) contains roughly <b>100,000 neurons</b>, with the number depending on the <b>density</b> of that part of the brain.","w":["10,000–12,000 is the number of connections one CNS neuron makes, not neurons per voxel.","The lecture figure was about 100,000 and it varies with density.","The number depends on the density of that part of the brain.","Far too low; the lecture figure was about 100,000."],"k":"~100,000 neurons per mm³, varying with density.","rn":[["","How many synapses are in the nervous system?","How many synapses are in the nervous system?","10^14; 100,000,000,000,000"]]},{"id":1022,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts neurons and glial cells?","o":["Neurons are excitable; glial cells are non-excitable","Glial cells are excitable; neurons are non-excitable","Both are excitable, but glia conduct more slowly","Neurons outnumber glia by about 10:1","Glia communicate with each other by electrical impulses"],"e":"<b>Neurons are excitable</b> (they can carry an electrical charge); <b>glia are non-excitable</b>. Glia are the <b>most numerous</b> cells in the CNS, outnumbering neurons about <b>10:1</b> (some evidence questions this).","w":["Reversed.","Glia are non-excitable.","It is glia that outnumber neurons.","Glia are non-excitable."],"k":"Neurons excitable; glia non-excitable and more numerous (~10:1).","rn":[["","What are the cell types in the nervous system?","What are the cell types in the nervous system?","Neurons (excitable); Glial cells (non-excitable)"]]},{"id":1023,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A signal is generated in the dendrites of neuron A, travels to its axon terminal and is then passed to neuron B. What form does the signal take within neuron A and between neurons A and B?","o":["Electrical within A; chemical between A and B","Chemical within A; electrical between A and B","Electrical in both cases","Chemical in both cases","Mechanical within A; chemical between A and B"],"e":"Communication <b>within an individual neuron</b> is by <b>electrical impulse</b>. Neurons broadly communicate <b>with other neurons</b> by <b>chemical transmission (neurotransmission)</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Between neurons is broadly chemical.","Within a neuron is electrical.","Not described in the lecture."],"k":"Within a neuron: electrical. Between neurons: chemical (neurotransmission).","rn":[["","Between neurons vs within a neuron: chemical or electrical?","Between neurons vs within a neuron: chemical or electrical?","Between neurons: chemical; Within a neuron: electrical"]]},{"id":1024,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a glial cell?","o":["Purkinje cell","Astrocyte","Oligodendrocyte","Microglia","Schwann cell"],"e":"The <b>Purkinje cell</b> is a <b>multipolar neuron</b> of the cerebellum. Glial cells include oligodendrocytes (CNS), Schwann cells (PNS), microglia and astrocytes.","w":["A CNS glial cell.","CNS myelinating glia.","CNS immune glia.","PNS glia."],"k":"Purkinje = neuron; the four glia named are oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells, microglia, astrocytes.","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of glial cell?","What are the 4 types of glial cell?","Oligodendrocytes (CNS); Schwann cells (PNS); Microglia; Astrocytes"]]},{"id":1025,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","f":"ns11","s":"An impulse starts at the part labelled A. Through which parts does it travel, in order?","o":["Dendrite → soma → axon → axon terminal","Axon → soma → dendrite → axon terminal","Soma → dendrite → axon → axon terminal","Dendrite → axon → soma → axon terminal","Axon terminal → axon → soma → dendrite"],"e":"Information flows <b>in at the dendrites and out at the terminal</b>: <b>dendrite</b> (detects, conducts toward soma) → <b>soma</b> → <b>axon</b> (away from soma) → <b>axon terminal</b> (releases neurotransmitter). Memory aid D-S-A-T.","w":["The axon conducts away from the soma.","Dendrites conduct toward the soma.","The axon arises from the soma.","This is the reverse direction."],"k":"D-S-A-T: dendrite → soma → axon → terminal.","img":{"src":"1/neuron","cap":"A myelinated multipolar neuron. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","Dendrite; Soma (cell body); Axon; Axon terminal; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1026,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Name the part labelled <b>C</b>, which contains the nucleus (B) and other organelles.","e":"C is the soma (cell body). Impulses travel dendrite (A) → soma → axon (F) → axon terminal (E).","k":"C = soma.","img":{"src":"1/neuron","cap":"A myelinated multipolar neuron. Labels removed."},"a":["soma","cell body","perikaryon"],"rn":[["","Soma","Soma","Cell body; Contains the organelles (e.g. nucleus)"],["","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","Dendrite; Soma (cell body); Axon; Axon terminal; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1027,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Vesicles release neurotransmitter into a synaptic gap from the part labelled <b>E</b>. Name it.","e":"E is the axon terminal, where vesicles release neurotransmitter onto the next cell.","k":"E = axon terminal.","img":{"src":"1/neuron","cap":"A myelinated multipolar neuron. Labels removed."},"a":["axon terminal","axon terminals","terminal","synaptic terminal","presynaptic terminal","synaptic bouton","bouton"],"rn":[["","What happens at the axon terminals?","What happens at the axon terminals?","Vesicles release neurotransmitter into the gap; It acts on the adjacent cell"],["","Axon Terminal","Axon Terminal","Contains neurotransmitters; Released into the synaptic gap"]]},{"id":1028,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"The axon is wrapped in the segments labelled <b>D</b>. How do they speed up conduction?","o":["The impulse jumps from node to node instead of travelling continuously","The impulse travels continuously but myelin boosts its amplitude","Myelin releases neurotransmitter at each node along the axon","Myelin converts the electrical impulse into a chemical signal","The impulse jumps between Schwann cells rather than between nodes"],"e":"The myelin sheath lets the impulse <b>jump from node to node</b> (saltatory conduction) instead of travelling continuously, giving much faster conduction, up to around <b>120 m/s</b>.","w":["Speed comes from jumping between nodes (saltatory conduction), not continuous travel.","Neurotransmitter is released at the axon terminal, not along the axon.","Within a neuron the signal stays electrical.","The impulse jumps from node to node (the gaps between myelin segments)."],"k":"Myelin → node-to-node (saltatory) conduction, up to ~120 m/s.","img":{"src":"1/neuron","cap":"A myelinated multipolar neuron. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","How does myelin speed up conduction?","How does myelin speed up conduction?","The impulse jumps from node to node; Instead of travelling continuously"]]},{"id":1029,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Approximately how fast can a myelinated axon conduct an impulse?","o":["Up to around 120 metres per second","Up to around 12 metres per second","Up to around 1,200 metres per second","Up to around 2.5 metres per second","Up to around 18 metres per second"],"e":"Myelination allows conduction up to around <b>120 m/s</b>, so messages arrive more quickly and efficiently.","w":["Too slow.","Too fast.","2.5 mm is the cortical thickness.","Not the figure given."],"k":"Myelinated conduction: up to ~120 m/s.","rn":[["","How fast can myelinated axons conduct?","How fast can myelinated axons conduct?","Up to about 120 metres per second"]]},{"id":1030,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which labelling of D, E and G is correct?","o":["D midbrain; E pons; G medulla oblongata","D pons; E midbrain; G medulla oblongata","D midbrain; E medulla oblongata; G pons","D thalamus; E pons; G medulla oblongata","D midbrain; E cerebellum; G pons"],"e":"Brainstem from top to bottom: midbrain (D), pons (E), medulla oblongata (G). It connects the brain to the spinal cord and controls vital functions, so damage is often fatal.","w":["The midbrain (D) lies above the pons (E).","The medulla oblongata is lowest, continuous with the spinal cord.","The thalamus is above the midbrain (B).","The cerebellum is F, behind the brainstem."],"k":"Brainstem: midbrain, pons, medulla.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What else is seen on the medial brain surface?","What else is seen on the medial brain surface?","Corpus callosum; Thalamus; Cerebrum; Hippocampus; Midbrain; Pons; Cerebellum; Medulla oblongata"],["","What does the hindbrain include?","What does the hindbrain include?","Cerebellum; Pons; Medulla oblongata"]]},{"id":1031,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A single cortical neuron is studied with tracing techniques. Approximately how many other neurons is it likely to connect with?","o":["Over 10,000–12,000","About 100,000 (one voxel)","About 100–120","About 1 million","About 86 billion"],"e":"CNS neurons are <b>deeply interconnected</b>: each connects with <b>over c. 10,000–12,000</b> other neurons. They do not exist in isolation.","w":["100,000 is the number of neurons in one cubic millimetre.","Far too few.","Too many.","That is the total number of neurons."],"k":"Each neuron connects with >10,000–12,000 others.","rn":[["","How many other neurons does each neuron connect with?","How many other neurons does each neuron connect with?","Over c. 10,000-12,000"]]},{"id":1032,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A patient with dementia shows marked atrophy of brain tissue on imaging. Why are the lost neurons not replaced?","o":["Neurons are post-mitotic and cannot proliferate","Microglia destroy any new neurons","Astrocytes block neuronal division by forming glial scars","Neurons can divide, but only in the spinal cord","The blood–brain barrier prevents new neurons from entering"],"e":"Neurons are <b>post-mitotic</b>: they <b>cannot proliferate</b>. If we lose them we do not get them back, which is why there is marked atrophy in dementia with no mechanism to replace the cells (apart from limited neurogenesis in neurogenic niches).","w":["Not stated.","Glial scars are part of repair; not given as the reason.","Not stated; the neurogenic niche named was the hippocampus.","Not the reason given."],"k":"Post-mitotic neurons are not replaced when lost.","rn":[["","Post-mitotic","Post-mitotic","Cannot proliferate (divide)"]]},{"id":1033,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"There is some evidence of adult neurogenesis in specific areas of the brain. What are these areas called, and what is an example?","o":["Neurogenic niches; the hippocampus","Neurogenic niches; the cerebellum","Neurogenic niches; the brainstem","Ganglia; the hippocampus","Ganglia; the dorsal root ganglion"],"e":"Areas of adult neurogenesis are the <b>neurogenic niches</b>. A well-known one is in the <b>hippocampus</b> (memory formation). Only a small number of new neurons are made there, tiny compared with 86 billion.","w":["The example given was the hippocampus (memory formation), not the cerebellum.","The example given was the hippocampus.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuronal cell bodies in the PNS, not a site of neurogenesis.","Ganglia are clusters of cell bodies; the areas of adult neurogenesis are neurogenic niches."],"k":"Neurogenic niche example: hippocampus.","rn":[["","Neurogenic Niches","Neurogenic Niches","Specific areas of the brain with some adult neurogenesis"],["","Where is a well-known neurogenic niche?","Where is a well-known neurogenic niche?","Hippocampus"]]},{"id":1034,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which statement correctly compares the two broad types of junction between an axon terminal and its target?","o":["Electrical are faster; chemical are slower and the majority","Chemical are faster; electrical are slower and the majority","Electrical are faster and the majority; chemical are rare","Chemical are faster and the majority; electrical are rare","Both are equally fast; chemical are the majority"],"e":"<b>Electrical synapses are faster</b>; <b>chemical synapses are slower</b> (though still fast) and form the <b>majority</b>. Fast electrical transmission is a survival advantage.","w":["Electrical synapses are the faster type.","Electrical are faster, but chemical synapses form the majority.","Chemical synapses are slower (though still fast) and form the majority.","Electrical synapses are faster than chemical ones."],"k":"Electrical = faster; chemical = slower, majority.","rn":[["","Electrical vs chemical synapses: speed","Electrical vs chemical synapses: speed","Electrical: faster; Chemical: slower"]]},{"id":1035,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Why is very fast electrical synaptic transmission advantageous?","o":["It provides a survival advantage","It allows neurons to divide","It removes excess potassium","It makes chemical synapses unnecessary","It forms the blood–brain barrier"],"e":"Electrical synapses give <b>really fast</b> transmission, which is a <b>survival advantage</b>.","w":["Neurons are post-mitotic.","K⁺ removal is an astrocyte function.","Chemical synapses are the majority.","The BBB involves astrocytes."],"k":"Fast electrical transmission = survival advantage.","rn":[["","Why are electrical synapses an advantage?","Why are electrical synapses an advantage?","Really fast transmission; Survival advantage"]]},{"id":1036,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Primary sensory neurons in the dorsal root ganglion have a single process that splits in two. Name this morphology.","e":"Dorsal root ganglion neurons are pseudo-unipolar: one process that bifurcates into peripheral and central branches.","k":"Pseudo-unipolar.","a":["pseudounipolar","pseudo-unipolar","pseudo unipolar","pseudounipolar neuron"]},{"id":1037,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Retinal neurons next to the photoreceptors have one axon and one dendrite. Name this morphology.","e":"Bipolar neurons (one axon, one dendrite) include the bipolar cells of the retina.","k":"Bipolar.","a":["bipolar","bipolar neuron","bipolar cell","bipolar cells"],"rn":[["","Bipolar Neuron","Bipolar Neuron","One axon and one dendrite"],["","Example of a bipolar neuron","Example of a bipolar neuron","Specialised sensory neurons; e.g. in the retina (bipolar retinal cells)"],["","What sits next to the bipolar retinal cells?","What sits next to the bipolar retinal cells?","Photoreceptors"]]},{"id":1038,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Name the cerebellar neuron with one axon and an enormous, extensive dendritic tree.","e":"Purkinje cells of the cerebellum are multipolar with huge dendritic trees.","k":"Purkinje cell.","a":["purkinje cell","purkinje","purkinje neuron","purkinje cells"],"rn":[["","What is special about a Purkinje cell?","What is special about a Purkinje cell?","A huge, extensive dendritic tree; For receiving incoming information"],["","Are Purkinje cells of the cerebellum the same as Purkinje fibres in the heart?","Are Purkinje cells of the cerebellum the same as Purkinje fibres in the heart?","No; Same (probably eponymous) name, different cells"]]},{"id":1039,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","f":"ns13","s":"Which pairing of multipolar neuron and its location is correct?","o":["Pyramidal neuron – hippocampus","Purkinje neuron – spinal cord","Motor neuron – cerebellum","Pyramidal neuron – retina","Purkinje neuron – dorsal root ganglion"],"e":"The three multipolar types to know: <b>motor neuron (spinal cord)</b>, <b>pyramidal neuron (hippocampus)</b> and <b>Purkinje neuron (cerebellum)</b>. Memory aid: “My Pet Purkinje”.","w":["Purkinje neurons are in the cerebellum.","Motor neurons are in the spinal cord.","The retina contains bipolar neurons.","DRG neurons are unipolar."],"k":"Motor–spinal cord; pyramidal–hippocampus; Purkinje–cerebellum.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of multipolar neuron (with locations)?","What are the 3 types of multipolar neuron (with locations)?","Motor neuron (spinal cord); Pyramidal neuron (hippocampus); Purkinje neuron (cerebellum); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1040,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"What is the most common neuron in the brain, and what is its morphology?","o":["The granule cell, which is multipolar","The Purkinje cell, which is multipolar","The pyramidal neuron, which is bipolar","The granule cell, which is unipolar","The motor neuron, which is bipolar"],"e":"The lecturer noted the <b>most common neuron in the brain is the granule cell</b>, which is also <b>multipolar</b>. Multipolar neurons are the majority of neurons in the brain.","w":["Purkinje cells are multipolar but not the most common.","Pyramidal neurons are multipolar.","Granule cells are multipolar.","Motor neurons are multipolar."],"k":"Most common brain neuron: granule cell (multipolar).","rn":[["","What is actually the most common neuron in the brain?","What is actually the most common neuron in the brain?","Granule cell (also multipolar)"]]},{"id":1041,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A neuron is described as having one axon and many dendrites. Which statement about this type is true?","o":["It is the most numerous type of neuron in the brain","It is found only in the retina","It is the type found in the dorsal root ganglia","It has no axon","It is a non-excitable cell"],"e":"<b>Multipolar</b> neurons have <b>one axon and multiple dendrites</b> and are the <b>majority</b> of neurons in the brain.","w":["Retinal sensory neurons are bipolar.","DRG neurons are unipolar.","It has one axon.","All neurons are excitable."],"k":"Multipolar = majority of brain neurons.","rn":[["","Multipolar Neuron","Multipolar Neuron","One axon and multiple dendrites; The majority of neurons in the brain"]]},{"id":1042,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"What does a ganglion consist of?","o":["A cluster of neuronal cell bodies","A bundle of myelinated axons","A group of glial cells around a blood vessel","A gap between two myelin segments","The largest groove on the brain surface"],"e":"A <b>ganglion</b> (plural ganglia) is a <b>cluster of neuronal cell bodies</b>, e.g. the dorsal root ganglion.","w":["That would be a tract (white matter).","This describes astrocyte end-feet at the BBB.","That is a node of Ranvier.","That is a fissure."],"k":"Ganglion = cluster of neuronal cell bodies.","rn":[["","Ganglion","Ganglion","A cluster of neuronal cell bodies; Plural: ganglia"]]},{"id":1043,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Neurons vary in morphology. What is the shape and size of a neuron linked to?","o":["The function of that neuron subtype","The age of the person","Whether it is in the CNS or PNS only","The number of glial cells around it","Whether it uses electrical or chemical synapses"],"e":"The slides state that the <b>shape and size is linked to the function</b> of that neuron subtype.","w":["Not stated.","Not the link given.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Morphology is linked to function.","rn":[["","What is the shape and size of a neuron linked to?","What is the shape and size of a neuron linked to?","Its function"]]},{"id":1044,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"The word “glia” comes from Greek. What does it mean, and what older view of glia does it reflect?","o":["“Glue”; glia were thought only to hold the brain together","“Glue”; glia were thought to conduct impulses like neurons","“Star”; glia were thought only to guide growing neurons","“Few processes”; glia were thought to be inactive","“Little brain”; glia were thought to control movement"],"e":"<b>Glia</b> is Greek for <b>“glue”</b>. We used to think they were just glue that held the brain together so neurons could do their job; we now know they do much more. They are non-excitable.","w":["Glia are non-excitable; the old view was that they were just glue.","“Star” is the root of astrocyte, not glia.","“Cell with few processes” is the meaning of oligodendrocyte.","“Little brain” is the meaning of cerebellum."],"k":"Glia = glue; non-excitable supporting cells.","rn":[["","What does \"glia\" mean?","What does \"glia\" mean?","Glue; Greek"]]},{"id":1045,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which three glial cell types are found in the CNS on neuroglia of the CNS?","o":["Astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and microglia","Astrocytes, Schwann cells and microglia","Oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells and astrocytes","Microglia, Purkinje cells and astrocytes","Schwann cells, oligodendrocytes and microglia"],"e":"The three CNS glia on the slide are <b>astrocyte</b>, <b>oligodendrocyte</b> and <b>microglia</b>. Schwann cells are the glia of the <b>PNS</b>.","w":["Schwann cells are PNS.","Schwann cells are PNS.","Purkinje cells are neurons.","Schwann cells are PNS."],"k":"CNS glia: astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia.","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of glial cell?","What are the 4 types of glial cell?","Oligodendrocytes (CNS); Schwann cells (PNS); Microglia; Astrocytes"]]},{"id":1046,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A CNS glial cell extends processes that myelinate axons of several different neurons at once. Name it.","e":"Oligodendrocytes myelinate many axons in the CNS; a Schwann cell myelinates a single axon in the PNS.","k":"Oligodendrocyte.","a":["oligodendrocyte","oligodendrocytes","oligodendroglia"],"rn":[["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"],["","How do you remember oligodendrocyte vs Schwann?","How do you remember oligodendrocyte vs Schwann?","Oligodendrocyte = Octopus (many arms, many axons); Schwann = Single"],["","Name 3 glial cells of the CNS (central nervous system).","Name 3 glial cells of the CNS (central nervous system).","Astrocyte; Oligodendrocyte; Microglia"]]},{"id":1047,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"The name “oligodendrocyte” is Greek. What does it mean, and who first described the cell?","o":["“Cell with few processes”; Pío del Río Hortega","“Star-shaped cell”; Santiago Ramón y Cajal","“Cell with few processes”; Santiago Ramón y Cajal","“Glue cell”; Pío del Río Hortega","“Branched cell”; Pío del Río Hortega"],"e":"Oligodendrocytes were first described by the Spanish anatomist <b>Pío del Río Hortega</b>; the name means a <b>“cell with few processes”</b>.","w":["Star-shaped = astrocyte; Cajal gave the neuronal doctrine.","Not Cajal.","“Glue” is glia.","Not the meaning given."],"k":"Oligodendrocyte = “cell with few processes”, del Río Hortega.","rn":[["","What does the name \"oligodendrocyte\" mean?","What does the name \"oligodendrocyte\" mean?","A cell with few processes; Greek"]]},{"id":1048,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Approximately what proportion of myelin is made up of myelin basic protein (MBP) and proteolipid protein (PLP) together?","o":["About 80%","About 20%","About 50%","About 5%","About 100%"],"e":"Myelin mostly consists of <b>MBP and PLP</b>: <b>c. 80%</b>. These are the two components you need to know.","w":["Too low.","Too low.","5% is the proportion of brain cells that are microglia.","Not all; there are other components."],"k":"MBP + PLP ≈ 80% of myelin.","rn":[["","What is myelin mostly made of?","What is myelin mostly made of?","Myelin basic protein (MBP); Proteolipid protein (PLP)"],["","MBP","MBP","Myelin Basic Protein"]]},{"id":1049,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"White matter in the CNS appears white because of a fatty substance. Which cell produces this substance there?","o":["Oligodendrocyte","Astrocyte","Microglia","Schwann cell","Granule cell"],"e":"Oligodendrocytes produce and maintain the <b>myelin sheath</b>, creating <b>white matter</b>. Myelin is fatty, which makes myelinated tracts look white.","w":["Astrocytes do not myelinate.","Microglia are immune cells.","Schwann cells myelinate in the PNS only.","A neuron."],"k":"CNS white matter = oligodendrocyte myelin.","rn":[["","Oligodendrocyte","Oligodendrocyte","CNS glial cell; Produces and maintains the myelin sheath; Each cell myelinates multiple axons"]]},{"id":1050,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Why does grey matter look grey rather than white?","o":["It consists mainly of cell bodies with little myelin","It consists mainly of myelinated axon tracts","It contains no glial cells of any kind","It is formed by Schwann cells rather than oligodendrocytes","It is made up mainly of astrocyte end-feet"],"e":"White matter is white because of fatty <b>myelin</b>; areas of <b>cell bodies</b> have little myelin and look <b>grey</b>, hence grey matter.","w":["Myelinated tracts look white, not grey.","Glia are present throughout the CNS.","Schwann cells are PNS glia; grey matter is grey because it has little myelin.","Grey colour is due to cell bodies with little myelin."],"k":"Grey matter = cell bodies, little myelin.","rn":[["","Why is grey matter grey?","Why is grey matter grey?","Areas of cell bodies with little myelin"]]},{"id":1051,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A pathogen enters brain tissue; a small, delicately branched resident immune cell engulfs it. Name the cell.","e":"Microglia are the brain's resident immune cells; they become activated and phagocytose pathogens and debris.","k":"Microglia.","a":["microglia","microglial cell","microglial cells","microglia cell"],"rn":[["","Microglia","Microglia","Resident immune cells of the brain; Phagocytic; About 5% of brain cells"],["","What activates microglia?","What activates microglia?","Pathogens"],["","What do activated microglia do to a pathogen?","What do activated microglia do to a pathogen?","Engulf it; Phagocytosis"]]},{"id":1052,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Microglia transform from a “quiescent” to an “activated” state. Why is “resting” a misleading description?","o":["They constantly scan their environment for threats","They are permanently in the activated state","They divide continuously while they are resting","They fire impulses even in the quiescent state","They only become active after the BBB is broken"],"e":"Although the first state is called resting, microglia are <b>never really resting</b>: they <b>constantly scan</b> their environment for pathogens or anything that could threaten neurons.","w":["They switch between quiescent and activated states.","Not stated; the point is they keep scanning their environment.","Glia are non-excitable.","Even quiescent microglia continuously scan for threats."],"k":"“Quiescent” microglia are still surveilling.","rn":[["","Are quiescent microglia really resting?","Are quiescent microglia really resting?","No; They constantly scan their environment for threats"]]},{"id":1053,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Why does the brain need its own resident immune cells?","o":["The BBB stops many molecules and immune cells entering the brain","The brain has no blood supply for immune cells to arrive by","Neurons divide constantly and need monitoring for errors","Astrocytes destroy any immune cells arriving in the blood","The meninges filter out all circulating white blood cells"],"e":"The <b>blood–brain barrier</b> is a selectively permeable barrier that stops many molecules (and immune cells) entering the brain, so the brain needs its <b>own immune cells</b>: the microglia.","w":["The brain has blood vessels; the barrier is the BBB.","Neurons are post-mitotic.","Astrocytes form the BBB; they do not destroy immune cells.","The lecture attributed this to the blood–brain barrier."],"k":"BBB limits immune cell entry → microglia are the brain's immune cells.","rn":[["","Why does the brain need its own immune cells?","Why does the brain need its own immune cells?","The blood-brain barrier stops many molecules and immune cells entering the brain"]]},{"id":1054,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which set of features correctly describes microglia?","o":["About 5% of brain cells, in regular mosaic patterns, promote repair","About 5% of brain cells, star-shaped, form the blood–brain barrier","About 5% of brain cells, myelinate several axons each","About 80% of brain cells, phagocytic, promote repair","Found mainly in the PNS, phagocytic, in mosaic patterns"],"e":"Microglia make up about <b>5%</b> of brain cells, are distributed in <b>regular mosaic patterns</b>, are <b>phagocytic and immune</b>, and <b>promote repair</b>.","w":["Star-shaped cells forming the BBB are astrocytes.","Myelinating multiple axons is the oligodendrocyte.","Microglia are about 5% of brain cells (80% of the cortex is used in cognition).","Microglia are the brain’s resident immune cells (CNS)."],"k":"Microglia: 5%, mosaic, phagocytic/immune, repair.","rn":[["","Key Features of Microglia","Key Features of Microglia","Network of delicately ramified (branched) cells; Regular mosaic pattern throughout the CNS; Promote repair"]]},{"id":1055,"t":"Neuroglia​","f":"ns20","s":"After a burst of neuronal activity, excess potassium accumulates outside neurons. Which glial cell helps remove it, and which function is this an example of?","o":["Astrocyte; maintaining the ionic environment","Astrocyte; neurotransmitter uptake","Astrocyte; structural support","Microglia; maintaining the ionic environment","Oligodendrocyte; maintaining the ionic environment"],"e":"<b>Astrocytes</b> maintain the <b>ionic environment</b>, for example by <b>removing excess K⁺</b>.","w":["Glutamate uptake is a separate astrocyte function; K⁺ removal is ionic balance.","Structural support is a separate astrocyte heading.","Microglia are immune cells; K⁺ removal is astrocytic.","Oligodendrocytes make CNS myelin."],"k":"Astrocytes remove excess K⁺ (ionic environment).","rn":[["","Example of astrocytes maintaining the ionic environment","Example of astrocytes maintaining the ionic environment","Removal of excess K+"]]},{"id":1056,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Glutamate released by neurons is taken up so that it does not build up around synapses. Which cell is mainly responsible?","o":["Astrocyte","Microglia","Oligodendrocyte","Purkinje cell","Schwann cell"],"e":"<b>Astrocytes</b> take up neurotransmitter (<b>glutamate</b>) released by neurons so it does not build up.","w":["Microglia are phagocytic immune cells.","Oligodendrocytes myelinate.","A neuron.","PNS myelinating cell."],"k":"Astrocytes: glutamate uptake.","rn":[["","Example of astrocyte neurotransmitter uptake","Example of astrocyte neurotransmitter uptake","Glutamate"]]},{"id":1057,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Astrocyte end-feet encapsulate blood vessels entering the brain. Name the structure they help form.","e":"Astrocyte end-feet help form the blood-brain barrier.","k":"Blood-brain barrier.","a":["blood-brain barrier","blood brain barrier","bbb"],"rn":[["","How do astrocytes form the blood-brain barrier?","How do astrocytes form the blood-brain barrier?","Encapsulate the blood vessels entering the brain; End-feet on capillaries"],["","BBB","BBB","Blood Brain Barrier"]]},{"id":1058,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a function of astrocytes listed?","o":["Phagocytosis of pathogens","Supply of glucose","Formation of glial scars","Structural support and pathfinding","Removal of excess K⁺"],"e":"Astrocyte functions: structural support (<b>pathfinding</b>), nutrients (<b>glucose</b>), ionic environment (<b>K⁺</b>), neurotransmitter uptake (<b>glutamate</b>), <b>repair (glial scars)</b> and the <b>BBB</b>. <b>Phagocytosis</b> of pathogens is a microglial function.","w":["An astrocyte function.","An astrocyte function (repair).","An astrocyte function.","An astrocyte function."],"k":"Phagocytosis = microglia; the rest are astrocyte jobs.","rn":[["","What do activated microglia do to a pathogen?","What do activated microglia do to a pathogen?","Engulf it; Phagocytosis"]]},{"id":1059,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Following injury to the brain, a glial scar forms. Which cell type forms it, and under which function heading?","o":["Astrocytes; repair of the nervous system","Astrocytes; structural support","Microglia; repair of the nervous system","Microglia; immune defence","Oligodendrocytes; repair of the nervous system"],"e":"Astrocytes carry out <b>repair</b> of the nervous system, for example the <b>formation of glial scars</b>.","w":["Structural support is a different astrocyte function; scars come under repair.","Microglia promote repair but glial scars are formed by astrocytes.","Glial scars are astrocytic.","Oligodendrocytes make CNS myelin."],"k":"Glial scar = astrocyte repair.","rn":[["","Name 3 glial cells of the CNS (central nervous system).","Name 3 glial cells of the CNS (central nervous system).","Astrocyte; Oligodendrocyte; Microglia"],["","What are the 2 anatomical divisions of the nervous system?","What are the 2 anatomical divisions of the nervous system?","Central nervous system (CNS); Peripheral nervous system (PNS)"],["","CNS","CNS","Central Nervous System"]]},{"id":1060,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Name the star-shaped glial cell that supplies neurons with glucose and takes up excess K⁺ and glutamate.","e":"Astrocytes support neurons metabolically, buffer K⁺, take up glutamate, form the blood-brain barrier and form glial scars after injury.","k":"Astrocyte.","a":["astrocyte","astrocytes","astroglia"],"rn":[["","Astrocyte","Astrocyte","Star-shaped glial cell; \"Remarkable cells\"; Forms the blood-brain barrier"],["","Example of astrocyte repair","Example of astrocyte repair","Formation of glial scars"]]},{"id":1061,"t":"Neuroglia​","f":"ns21","s":"An electron micrograph of a peripheral nerve shows dark concentric bands wrapped around one axon by a single glial cell. Which statement about this glial cell is correct?","o":["It myelinates a single axon and supports axon regeneration","It myelinates several axons and supports axon regeneration","It myelinates a single axon and forms the blood–brain barrier","It myelinates several axons and is found only in the CNS","It is a phagocytic cell that wraps damaged axons"],"e":"This is a <b>Schwann cell</b>, the glia of the <b>PNS</b>. It myelinates axons by <b>spiral wrapping</b> (the dark bands are the layers of myelin), <b>a single Schwann cell myelinates a single axon</b>, and it is important for <b>regeneration</b>, providing a substrate for the axon to grow along.","w":["One Schwann cell myelinates one axon.","The BBB is formed by astrocytes.","That describes the oligodendrocyte, not a PNS cell.","Phagocytic glia are microglia (CNS)."],"k":"Schwann cell: PNS, one axon, spiral wrapping, supports regeneration.","rn":[["","Schwann Cell","Schwann Cell","Neuroglia of the PNS; Myelinates a single axon; Spiral wrapping"],["","Which myelinating cell supports regeneration?","Which myelinating cell supports regeneration?","Schwann cell"]]},{"id":1062,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A peripheral nerve axon is cut and begins to regrow along the path of its old sheath. Which property of the PNS glia makes this possible?","o":["Schwann cells provide a substrate for the axon to grow along","Oligodendrocytes provide a substrate for the axon to grow along","Astrocytes form a glial scar that guides the axon back","Microglia clear a path for the axon by phagocytosis","The neuron divides to replace the lost part of the axon"],"e":"Schwann cells are important for <b>regeneration</b>: they provide a <b>substrate for the axon to grow along</b>.","w":["Oligodendrocytes are CNS only.","Glial scars form in the CNS after injury; they are not the regeneration substrate.","Microglia are CNS immune cells; regeneration was attributed to Schwann cells.","Neurons are post-mitotic."],"k":"Schwann cells support axon regeneration.","rn":[["","Why are Schwann cells important for regeneration?","Why are Schwann cells important for regeneration?","Provide a substrate for the axon to grow along"]]},{"id":1063,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"What is the key difference between oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells?","o":["Schwann cell: one axon; oligodendrocyte: many axons","Schwann cell: many axons; oligodendrocyte: one axon","Schwann cell: CNS; oligodendrocyte: PNS","Schwann cell: excitable; oligodendrocyte: non-excitable","Schwann cell: no myelin; oligodendrocyte: myelin"],"e":"The key difference: a <b>Schwann cell myelinates a single axon</b> (PNS), while an <b>oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons</b> (CNS) simultaneously. Memory aid: Oligodendrocyte = Octopus (many arms); Schwann = Single.","w":["Reversed: Oligodendrocyte = Octopus (many); Schwann = Single.","Reversed: Schwann cells are PNS, oligodendrocytes CNS.","All glia are non-excitable.","Both make myelin."],"k":"Schwann = single axon (PNS); oligodendrocyte = multiple axons (CNS).","rn":[["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"],["","How do you remember oligodendrocyte vs Schwann?","How do you remember oligodendrocyte vs Schwann?","Oligodendrocyte = Octopus (many arms, many axons); Schwann = Single"]]},{"id":1064,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Name the short gap between two adjacent segments of myelin on an axon.","e":"Nodes of Ranvier are gaps between myelin segments; the impulse jumps from node to node.","k":"Node of Ranvier.","a":["node of ranvier","nodes of ranvier","node","ranvier node"],"rn":[["","Node of Ranvier","Node of Ranvier","Gap between myelin segments"]]},{"id":1065,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A disease damages only the myelinating cells of the CNS. Which of the following would you expect to remain <b>unaffected</b>?","o":["Myelin around peripheral nerve axons","Myelin in the spinal cord","White matter of the cerebral hemispheres","The corpus callosum","The white matter of the arbor vitae"],"e":"CNS myelin is made by <b>oligodendrocytes</b>; PNS myelin is made by <b>Schwann cells</b>. Damage limited to CNS myelinating cells spares <b>peripheral nerve</b> myelin. The spinal cord, cerebral white matter, the corpus callosum (a white-matter tract) and cerebellar white matter are all CNS.","w":["The spinal cord is CNS.","Cerebral white matter is CNS.","A CNS white-matter tract.","Cerebellar white matter is CNS."],"k":"CNS myelin (oligodendrocytes) vs PNS myelin (Schwann cells).","rn":[["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"]]},{"id":1066,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which of the following best describes the role of the peripheral nervous system?","o":["Linking the CNS with the viscera, muscles, skin and glands","Processing of motor and sensory information in the cortex","Communication between the two cerebral hemispheres","Coordination of balance, posture and skilled voluntary movement","Control of respiration and the cardiovascular system"],"e":"The PNS provides <b>communication between the CNS and the periphery</b>, i.e. <b>viscera, muscles, joints, vessels, skin and glands</b>. Peripheral nerves convey motor, sensory and autonomic axons.","w":["That is the cortex (CNS).","That is the corpus callosum.","That is the cerebellum.","That is the brainstem."],"k":"PNS connects CNS to periphery.","rn":[["","What does \"periphery\" include?","What does \"periphery\" include?","Viscera; Muscles; Joints; Vessels; Skin; Glands"],["","What does the PNS supply nerves to?","What does the PNS supply nerves to?","Muscles; Glands; Skin; Vessels; Viscera"]]},{"id":1067,"t":"Central Nervous System","f":"ns24","s":"Which structure is <b>not</b> part of the forebrain?","o":["Cerebellum","Thalamus","Hypothalamus","Left cerebral hemisphere","Right cerebral hemisphere"],"e":"The <b>forebrain</b> includes the <b>cerebral hemispheres, thalamus and hypothalamus</b>. The <b>cerebellum</b> is part of the <b>hindbrain</b> (with pons and medulla oblongata).","w":["Forebrain.","Forebrain.","Forebrain.","Forebrain."],"k":"Forebrain = hemispheres, thalamus, hypothalamus; cerebellum = hindbrain.","rn":[["","What does the hindbrain include?","What does the hindbrain include?","Cerebellum; Pons; Medulla oblongata"]]},{"id":1068,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Name the division labelled <b>D</b>, the interface between forebrain and hindbrain.","e":"D is the midbrain, part of the brainstem with the pons (E) and medulla oblongata (G).","k":"D = midbrain.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"a":["midbrain","mesencephalon"],"rn":[["","What is the midbrain?","What is the midbrain?","The interface between the forebrain and the hindbrain; Part of the brainstem"],["","What are the 3 divisions of the brain?","What are the 3 divisions of the brain?","Forebrain; Midbrain; Hindbrain"],["","What forms the brainstem?","What forms the brainstem?","Midbrain; Hindbrain"]]},{"id":1069,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Decades ago, surgeons cut the white-matter structure labelled <b>A</b> so the two hemispheres could no longer coordinate. Name it.","e":"A is the corpus callosum, the large white-matter tract linking the two cerebral hemispheres; when cut, the hemispheres could no longer act in a coordinated way.","k":"A = corpus callosum.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"a":["corpus callosum","callosum"],"rn":[["","What happens if the corpus callosum is cut?","What happens if the corpus callosum is cut?","The two hemispheres can no longer act in a coordinated way"],["","What connects the two cerebral hemispheres?","What connects the two cerebral hemispheres?","Corpus callosum; Large white matter tract"]]},{"id":1070,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Name the paired forebrain relay structure labelled <b>B</b>.","e":"B is the thalamus, a paired relay through which information passes to the cortex.","k":"B = thalamus.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"a":["thalamus","thalami"],"rn":[["","What does the thalamus do?","What does the thalamus do?","Acts as a relay through which information passes"]]},{"id":1071,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"On the surface of the brain, which term is used for the largest grooves?","o":["Fissures","Sulci","Gyri","Foramina","Ganglia"],"e":"<b>Gyri</b> are ridges and <b>sulci</b> are depressions; the <b>largest grooves</b> are called <b>fissures</b> (e.g. the lateral fissure).","w":["Sulci are depressions, but the largest are fissures.","Gyri are ridges.","A foramen is an opening or passage.","Ganglia are clusters of cell bodies."],"k":"Largest grooves = fissures."},{"id":1072,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A question refers to the “lateral sulcus”, but you learned the term “lateral fissure”. What should you conclude?","o":["They are alternative names for the same landmark","The lateral sulcus is a smaller groove next to the lateral fissure","The lateral sulcus separates the frontal and parietal lobes","The lateral sulcus is only seen in dissection","The lateral sulcus is part of the cerebellum"],"e":"The largest grooves, e.g. the <b>lateral fissure</b>, are also referred to as the <b>lateral sulcus</b>; questions may use another name too. These are just <b>alternative names</b>.","w":["They are the same.","The central sulcus separates frontal and parietal lobes.","Not stated.","It is a cerebral landmark."],"k":"Lateral fissure = lateral sulcus (alternative names)."},{"id":1073,"t":"Central Nervous System","f":"ns26","s":"Which landmark separates the lobe containing the primary motor cortex from the lobe containing the primary somatosensory cortex?","o":["Central sulcus","Lateral fissure","Corpus callosum","Arbor vitae","Hippocampus"],"e":"The <b>central sulcus</b> separates the <b>frontal lobe</b> (primary motor cortex, in front) from the <b>parietal lobe</b> (primary somatosensory cortex, behind). It is easy to see on images but much harder in dissection.","w":["Not the motor/somatosensory boundary given.","A white-matter tract between hemispheres.","Cerebellar white matter.","Memory structure in the temporal lobe."],"k":"Central sulcus: frontal (motor) in front, parietal (somatosensory) behind.","rn":[["","What does the central sulcus separate?","What does the central sulcus separate?","Frontal lobe (motor, in front); Parietal lobe (somatosensory, behind)"]]},{"id":1074,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient has a stroke affecting the cortex just behind the central sulcus. Which function is most likely to be impaired?","o":["Touch, pain and proprioception","Planning of voluntary movement","Hearing and language comprehension","Visual processing and recognition","Formation of new memories"],"e":"Just behind the central sulcus is the <b>parietal lobe</b> with the <b>primary somatosensory cortex</b>: <b>pain, touch and proprioception</b>.","w":["Movement is the primary motor cortex, in front of the sulcus.","Hearing is the temporal lobe.","Vision is the occipital lobe.","New memories are the hippocampus."],"k":"Parietal = somatosensory (pain, touch, proprioception).","rn":[["","What are the functions of the Parietal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Parietal Lobe?","Primary somatosensory cortex; Pain, touch and proprioception"]]},{"id":1075,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A railway worker survives an injury damaging the front of his brain. Afterwards, his ability to think, consider and plan is changed. Which lobe was most likely damaged?","o":["Frontal","Parietal","Temporal","Occipital","Cerebellum"],"e":"The <b>frontal lobe</b> contains the primary motor cortex and is responsible for <b>executive functions</b> and the ability to <b>think and consider</b>. Lesion studies like this famous case let us map functional specificity.","w":["Somatosensory.","Auditory, olfactory, memory.","Visual.","Not a cerebral lobe; coordination."],"k":"Frontal lobe: motor, executive, thinking and considering.","rn":[["","What are the functions of the Frontal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Frontal Lobe?","Primary motor cortex; Executive functions; Ability to think and consider"]]},{"id":1076,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient develops altered hearing and sense of smell after a single cortical lesion. Name the lobe labelled <b>D</b>, the likely site.","e":"D is the temporal lobe: primary auditory and olfactory cortex, and implicated in learning and memory (hippocampus on its medial aspect).","k":"D = temporal.","img":{"src":"1/lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Labels removed."},"a":["temporal","temporal lobe"],"rn":[["","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","Primary auditory cortex; Primary olfactory (smell) cortex; Learning and memory"]]},{"id":1077,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A tumour in the region labelled <b>C</b> would most likely disturb which function?","o":["Vision","Hearing","Voluntary movement","Touch","Satiety"],"e":"The <b>occipital lobe</b>, at the back, contains the <b>primary visual cortex</b>.","w":["Temporal.","Frontal (primary motor cortex).","Parietal.","Hypothalamus."],"k":"Occipital = primary visual cortex.","img":{"src":"1/lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","How do you remember the lobe functions?","How do you remember the lobe functions?","Frontal = Function (executive) + movement; Parietal = Pain / touch; Temporal = Tunes (hearing) + memory; Occipital = Optics (vision)"]]},{"id":1078,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"How have we learned most of what we know about which lobe does what?","o":["Lesion studies of people with injury in known locations","Counting the number of neurons in each lobe","Measuring the conduction velocity of each lobe","Comparing the thickness of the cortex between lobes","Tracing the connections of single cortical neurons"],"e":"Much of what we know comes from <b>lesion studies</b>: when someone has a brain injury in a known location, the resulting loss of function lets us <b>map functional specificity</b>.","w":["Neuron numbers do not reveal function.","Not described; function was mapped from lesions.","Cortical thickness (about 2.5 mm) does not reveal lobe function.","Tracing showed each neuron connects to 10,000–12,000 others, not lobe function."],"k":"Functional specificity mapped by lesion studies.","rn":[["","How do we know about functional specificity?","How do we know about functional specificity?","Lesion studies; Brain injury in a known location shows which function is lost"]]},{"id":1079,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"After damage to one cortical area, a patient gradually regains some function as other areas take over. What limitation of “functional specificity” does this illustrate?","o":["It underplays the plasticity of the brain","It overstates how many neurons each lobe contains","It assumes that neurons can divide and replace each other","It ignores the role of the corpus callosum","It applies only to the cerebellum and brainstem"],"e":"The lecturer cautioned that functional specificity <b>underplays plasticity</b>. The brain is changeable, so if one area is damaged, <b>other areas can compensate</b>; it is not one-area-one-function.","w":["The criticism was about plasticity, not neuron counts.","Compensation is by other areas, not new neurons (neurons are post-mitotic).","The point was that other areas can take over (plasticity).","Functional specificity refers to cortical lobes."],"k":"Plasticity: other areas can compensate.","rn":[["","What does functional specificity underplay?","What does functional specificity underplay?","Plasticity; Other areas can compensate if one is damaged; Particularly in young people"]]},{"id":1080,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient cannot form new memories after damage to the medial aspect of the temporal lobe, but old memories are preserved. Which structure is most likely damaged, and why are old memories spared?","o":["Hippocampus; long-term storage happens elsewhere in the cortex","Hippocampus; old memories are stored in the cerebellum","Amygdala; long-term storage happens elsewhere in the cortex","Hypothalamus; old memories are stored in the brainstem","Hippocampus; it stores all long-term memories itself"],"e":"The <b>hippocampus</b>, on the <b>medial</b> temporal lobe, is responsible for the <b>formation and consolidation of new memories</b> (consolidating during sleep). <b>Longer-term storage happens elsewhere in the cortex</b>, so older memories can be spared.","w":["Longer-term storage is elsewhere in the cortex.","Medial temporal memory formation is the hippocampus.","The hypothalamus controls ANS, endocrine, thermoregulation and satiety.","If it stored everything, old memories would be lost too."],"k":"Hippocampus forms/consolidates new memories; long-term storage is elsewhere.","rn":[["","Where are long-term memories stored?","Where are long-term memories stored?","Elsewhere in the cortex"]]},{"id":1081,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A student writes that the limbic lobe is the fifth lobe of the brain. Which correction is accurate?","o":["It is not really a lobe but a functional network","It is a lobe of the cerebellum, not the cerebrum","It is another name for the medial temporal lobe","It is part of the brainstem, not the cortex","It is a white-matter tract joining the hemispheres"],"e":"You may read about the <b>limbic lobe/limbic system</b>, our “<b>emotional core</b>”. It is <b>not really a lobe</b> but a <b>functional network</b>.","w":["It is a functional network, the emotional core.","It is described as a network, not a renaming of one lobe.","It is not part of the brainstem.","That is the corpus callosum."],"k":"Limbic “lobe” = functional network (emotional core).","rn":[["","Limbic System","Limbic System","Our \"emotional core\"; Not really a lobe; A functional network"]]},{"id":1082,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the cerebral cortex is correct?","o":["About 2.5 mm thick with 10–20 billion neurons","About 25 mm thick with 10–20 billion neurons","About 2.5 mm thick with 86 billion neurons","About 25 mm thick with 86 billion neurons","About 2.5 mm thick with about 100,000 neurons"],"e":"The cortex is the outer layer of <b>grey matter</b>, <b>2.5 mm thick</b> with <b>10–20 billion neurons</b>. It was the <b>last</b> part of the brain to evolve, and c. <b>80%</b> of it functions in cognition.","w":["The cortex is about 2.5 mm thick.","86 billion is the whole nervous system.","Both values are wrong.","100,000 is roughly one cubic millimetre (voxel)."],"k":"Cortex: GM, 2.5 mm, 10–20 billion neurons, last to evolve.","rn":[["","Cerebral Cortex","Cerebral Cortex","Outer layer of grey matter of the cerebral hemispheres; 10-20 billion neurons; 2.5 mm thick"]]},{"id":1083,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"The primary function of the cerebral cortex is to process motor and sensory information. Yet approximately what proportion of it functions in cognition (e.g. Language, following instructions)?","o":["About 80%","About 20%","About 5%","About 50%","About 10%"],"e":"Although its primary function is processing <b>motor and sensory</b> information, about <b>80%</b> of the cortex functions in <b>cognition</b>.","w":["Too low.","Too low.","Too low.","Too low."],"k":"~80% of cortex = cognition.","rn":[["","What percentage of the cortex functions in cognition?","What percentage of the cortex functions in cognition?","c. 80%"]]},{"id":1084,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"The cerebral cortex is often compared to a crumpled sheet of paper. What does this analogy explain?","o":["It is highly folded to fit into the skull, giving gyri and sulci","It is made of many separate sheets of neurons stacked together","It is grey matter on the inside covered by white matter","It is thicker over the frontal lobe than the occipital lobe","It unfolds during development as the skull grows"],"e":"The cortex is <b>highly folded to fit into the skull</b>, hence gyri and sulci, like a crumpled sheet of paper.","w":["It is one folded sheet.","The cortex is grey matter on the outside.","The analogy is about folding, not thickness.","The point is folding to fit inside the skull."],"k":"Folding lets a large cortex fit in the skull.","rn":[["","Why is the cerebral cortex highly folded?","Why is the cerebral cortex highly folded?","To fit into the skull; Hence gyri and sulci"]]},{"id":1085,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A tumour at <b>C</b>, just below B, causes problems with temperature control and appetite. Name structure C.","e":"C is the hypothalamus: temperature, hunger/satiety, thirst and circadian rhythms; it links the nervous and endocrine systems through the pituitary.","k":"C = hypothalamus.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"a":["hypothalamus"],"rn":[["","What does the hypothalamus contribute to controlling?","What does the hypothalamus contribute to controlling?","Autonomic nervous system; Endocrine system; Thermoregulation; Satiety; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1086,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> one of the four things the hypothalamus contributes to controlling?","o":["Coordination of movement","Autonomic nervous system","Endocrine system","Thermoregulation","Satiety (feeling full)"],"e":"The hypothalamus contributes to control of the <b>ANS</b>, <b>endocrine system</b>, <b>thermoregulation</b> and <b>satiety</b> (memory aid HEAT). Coordinating movement is the job of the <b>cerebellum</b>.","w":["Hypothalamus controls the ANS (HEAT).","Hypothalamus controls endocrine function (HEAT).","Hypothalamus controls thermoregulation (HEAT).","Hypothalamus controls satiety."],"k":"HEAT: Hunger, Endocrine, Autonomic, Temperature.","rn":[["","Cerebellum","Cerebellum","Latin for \"little brain\"; Coordination and modification of muscular activity"],["","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","Motor movements; Regulating sensory systems; Balance / posture"],["","When do new memories consolidate?","When do new memories consolidate?","During sleep; Rapid eye movement (REM) sleep"]]},{"id":1087,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Through which gland does the hypothalamus link the nervous and endocrine systems?","e":"The hypothalamus controls the pituitary gland, linking nervous and endocrine systems.","k":"Pituitary gland.","a":["pituitary","pituitary gland","hypophysis"],"rn":[["","How does the hypothalamus link to the endocrine system?","How does the hypothalamus link to the endocrine system?","Via the infundibulum (downward growth); Which gives rise to the pituitary gland"]]},{"id":1088,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"“Satiety” is one of the functions of the hypothalamus. What does this term mean?","o":["Suppression of hunger","Regulation of body temperature","Control of heart rate","Release of hormones","Detection of threats"],"e":"<b>Satiety</b> = the suppression of hunger.","w":["Thermoregulation.","Not satiety.","Endocrine.","Not satiety."],"k":"Satiety = suppression of hunger.","rn":[["","Satiety","Satiety","Suppression of hunger"]]},{"id":1089,"t":"Central Nervous System","f":"ns29","s":"A patient sustains severe damage to the brainstem. Why is this most likely to be fatal?","o":["It controls respiration and the cardiovascular system","It produces most of the myelin used in the central nervous system","It contains the primary somatosensory cortex","It forms and consolidates all new memories","It controls the endocrine system and satiety"],"e":"The brainstem <b>connects the cortex to the spinal cord</b> and <b>controls respiration and the cardiovascular system</b>. Damage is most likely <b>incompatible with life</b> because of the inability to regulate these.","w":["Myelin is made by oligodendrocytes.","That is the parietal lobe.","That is the hippocampus.","That is the hypothalamus."],"k":"Brainstem: respiration + CVS → damage likely fatal.","rn":[["","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Connects the cortex to the spinal cord; Controls respiration and the cardiovascular system"]]},{"id":1090,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"The brainstem is formed by which divisions of the brain?","o":["Midbrain and hindbrain","Forebrain and midbrain","Forebrain and hindbrain","Thalamus and hypothalamus","Cerebellum and cerebral hemispheres"],"e":"The <b>brainstem</b> is formed by the <b>midbrain and hindbrain</b> (pons and medulla oblongata). It links the brain to the spinal cord.","w":["The forebrain is not brainstem.","The forebrain is not brainstem.","Both forebrain.","Not brainstem."],"k":"Brainstem = midbrain + hindbrain (pons, medulla).","rn":[["","What forms the brainstem?","What forms the brainstem?","Midbrain; Hindbrain"],["","Brainstem","Brainstem","Formed by the midbrain and hindbrain; Functionally critical"]]},{"id":1091,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A message from the motor cortex must reach a motor neuron in the spinal cord. Which part of the brain provides the connection between the two?","o":["Brainstem","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Corpus callosum","Hippocampus"],"e":"The brainstem is functionally critical: it <b>connects the cortex to the spinal cord</b>.","w":["Coordination.","Homeostasis.","Connects the two hemispheres.","Memory."],"k":"Brainstem connects cortex to spinal cord.","rn":[["","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Connects the cortex to the spinal cord; Controls respiration and the cardiovascular system"]]},{"id":1092,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Name the tree-like appearance of white matter seen in a sagittal section of the cerebellum.","e":"Arbor vitae (“tree of life”): grey matter tightly packed over branching white matter.","k":"Arbor vitae.","a":["arbor vitae","arbour vitae","tree of life"],"rn":[["","Arbor Vitae","Arbor Vitae","\"Tree of life\"; Appearance of cerebellar grey matter overlaying white matter"]]},{"id":1093,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient sways when standing and walks unsteadily, with normal strength and sensation. Name the structure labelled <b>F</b>, the most likely site.","e":"F is the cerebellum (“little brain”): coordination and balance. Its white matter branches like a tree (arbor vitae).","k":"F = cerebellum.","img":{"src":"1/midsag","cap":"Midsagittal section of the brain. Labels removed."},"a":["cerebellum"],"rn":[["","Cerebellum","Cerebellum","Latin for \"little brain\"; Coordination and modification of muscular activity"],["","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","Motor movements; Regulating sensory systems; Balance / posture"]]},{"id":1094,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"How has understanding of the cerebellum changed compared with teaching 10–15 years ago?","o":["Once seen as purely motor; now evidence for a role in cognition","Once seen as cognitive; now known to be purely motor","Once seen as purely motor; now known to control breathing","Once placed in the forebrain; now placed in the midbrain","Once thought to be grey matter only; now known to have white matter"],"e":"10–15 years ago the cerebellum would have been described as <b>purely motor</b>; there is now <b>growing evidence for a role in cognition</b>, as it connects with the <b>frontal cortex</b>.","w":["Reversed: the shift is from motor-only to also cognitive.","Breathing is the brainstem.","The cerebellum is hindbrain.","This was not the change described."],"k":"Cerebellum: growing evidence of cognitive role (links with frontal cortex).","rn":[["","Is the cerebellum purely motor?","Is the cerebellum purely motor?","No; Growing evidence of a role in cognition; Connects with the frontal cortex"]]},{"id":1095,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"What does the Latin word “cerebellum” mean?","o":["Little brain","Tree of life","Horse's tail","Glue","Star"],"e":"<b>Cerebellum</b> = Latin for “<b>little brain</b>”.","w":["Arbor vitae.","Cauda equina.","Glia.","Astro (astrocyte)."],"k":"Cerebellum = little brain.","rn":[["","Cerebellum","Cerebellum","Latin for \"little brain\"; Coordination and modification of muscular activity"]]},{"id":1096,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"How does the arrangement of grey and white matter in the cerebellum compare with the cerebral cortex?","o":["Different: tightly packed grey over white, tree-like in section","Identical to the cerebral cortex in arrangement","Different: white matter on the outside, grey matter inside","Different: the cerebellum has no white matter at all","Different: grey matter only, no tree-like pattern"],"e":"The cerebellar cortex is <b>tightly packed grey matter overlaying white matter</b>, which is <b>different from the cerebral cortex</b>; in section it looks like a leaf or tree (arbor vitae).","w":["It is described as different from the cerebral cortex.","Grey matter overlies white matter.","The white matter forms the arbor vitae.","It looks like a tree (arbor vitae) in section."],"k":"Cerebellum: packed GM over branching WM (different from cerebral cortex).","rn":[["","What does the cerebellar cortex consist of?","What does the cerebellar cortex consist of?","Tightly packed grey matter; Overlaying white matter"],["","How does the cerebellar cortex differ from the cerebral cortex?","How does the cerebellar cortex differ from the cerebral cortex?","Different arrangement; Looks like a leaf or tree in section"]]},{"id":1097,"t":"Central Nervous System","f":"ns31","s":"Between which vertebral levels does the spinal cord typically extend?","o":["From the atlas (C1) to L1","From the atlas (C1) to the sacrum","From the skull base to T12","From C2 to L5","From C1 to the coccyx"],"e":"The spinal cord extends from the <b>atlas (C1)</b> to the <b>first lumbar vertebra (L1)</b>, within the <b>vertebral canal</b>. In adults it ends around L1–L2; in children it extends lower.","w":["It ends around L1–L2 in adults.","It continues below T12.","It starts at C1 and ends around L1.","It ends around L1."],"k":"Spinal cord: C1 → L1 (around L1–L2 in adults).","rn":[["","Where does the spinal cord extend from and to?","Where does the spinal cord extend from and to?","Atlas (C1); To the first lumbar vertebra (L1)"]]},{"id":1098,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Compared with an adult, where would you expect the lower end of the spinal cord to lie in a child?","o":["Lower","Higher","At exactly the same level","At C1","Outside the vertebral canal"],"e":"There is no uniformity: in adults the cord ends around <b>L1–L2</b>, and in <b>children it extends lower</b>.","w":["It is lower.","Not uniform.","That is the upper end.","It is in the vertebral canal."],"k":"Children: spinal cord extends lower.","rn":[["","Where does the spinal cord end in children?","Where does the spinal cord end in children?","Lower than in adults"]]},{"id":1099,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Name the space, bounded in front by the vertebral body, in which the spinal cord lies.","e":"The spinal cord lies in the vertebral canal, extending from the foramen magnum to about L1–L2 in adults (lower in children).","k":"Vertebral canal.","a":["vertebral canal","spinal canal","vertebral foramen"],"rn":[["","Where is the spinal cord located?","Where is the spinal cord located?","Vertebral canal"],["","What is seen on a spinal cord cross-section?","What is seen on a spinal cord cross-section?","Vertebral body; Vertebral canal; Spinal cord; Spinous process"]]},{"id":1100,"t":"Peripheral Nervous System","s":"Which component of the PNS controls skeletal muscle and is under voluntary control?","o":["Somatic","Autonomic","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic","Enteric"],"e":"The PNS has two components: <b>somatic (voluntary)</b>, controlling <b>skeletal muscles</b> and joints; and <b>autonomic (involuntary)</b>, controlling organs, vessels and autonomic responses.","w":["Involuntary.","A division of the autonomic component.","A division of the autonomic component.","Not covered."],"k":"Somatic = voluntary, skeletal muscle.","rn":[["","Somatic Component","Somatic Component","Voluntary; Skeletal muscles, muscles, joints"],["","What are the 2 broad components of the PNS?","What are the 2 broad components of the PNS?","Somatic (voluntary); Autonomic (involuntary)"]]},{"id":1101,"t":"Peripheral Nervous System","s":"A peripheral nerve is found to contain motor, sensory and autonomic axons. Why is it difficult to separate somatic and autonomic components anatomically?","o":["Many peripheral nerves contain axons from both components","Autonomic axons travel only in the cranial nerves","Somatic and autonomic axons fuse within the nerve","Autonomic axons are found only in the CNS","Somatic axons all lose their myelin in the periphery"],"e":"<b>Many peripheral nerves contain axons from both components</b>, so they are not always anatomically separate. Spinal nerves are <b>mixed</b>: motor, sensory and autonomic fibres.","w":["Spinal nerves also carry autonomic fibres.","They remain separate axons in a mixed nerve.","Autonomic is a component of the PNS.","Not described; the point is mixed nerves."],"k":"Peripheral nerves are often mixed (somatic + autonomic).","rn":[["","Are the somatic and autonomic components always anatomically separate?","Are the somatic and autonomic components always anatomically separate?","No; Many peripheral nerves contain axons from both"]]},{"id":1102,"t":"Peripheral Nervous System","s":"Which pair correctly matches a type of peripheral nerve with where it originates?","o":["Cranial – brain or brainstem; spinal – spinal cord","Cranial – spinal cord; spinal – brain or brainstem","Cranial – brainstem only; spinal – dorsal root ganglion","Cranial – cerebellum; spinal – spinal cord","Cranial – brain or brainstem; spinal – vertebral bodies"],"e":"Peripheral nerves arise from the <b>brain or brainstem</b> as <b>cranial nerves</b>, or from the <b>spinal cord</b> as <b>spinal nerves</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Spinal nerves arise from the spinal cord.","Cranial nerves arise from the brain or brainstem.","They arise from the spinal cord, not bone."],"k":"Cranial nerves: brain/brainstem. Spinal nerves: spinal cord.","rn":[["","Where do all peripheral nerves arise from?","Where do all peripheral nerves arise from?","Brain or brainstem (cranial nerves); Spinal cord (spinal nerves)"]]},{"id":1103,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which of the following would be controlled mainly by the autonomic component of the PNS?","o":["Diameter of blood vessels","Flexing the elbow to pick up a cup","Kicking a ball","Moving the eyes voluntarily to read","Clenching a fist"],"e":"The <b>autonomic (involuntary)</b> component controls <b>organs, vessels and autonomic responses</b>; you don't think about it (e.g. the heart). The other options are voluntary skeletal muscle actions (somatic).","w":["Voluntary (somatic).","Voluntary (somatic).","Voluntary (somatic).","Voluntary (somatic)."],"k":"Autonomic: organs, vessels; somatic: voluntary skeletal muscle.","rn":[["","How do astrocytes form the blood-brain barrier?","How do astrocytes form the blood-brain barrier?","Encapsulate the blood vessels entering the brain; End-feet on capillaries"]]},{"id":1104,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A medical student learning to take blood first has to think through every step, then begins linking the thinking with the doing, and eventually performs it without thinking. Which sequence of motor-learning stages does this describe?","o":["Cognitive → associative → autonomous","Associative → cognitive → autonomous","Autonomous → associative → cognitive","Cognitive → autonomous → associative","Sensory → motor → autonomic"],"e":"From the Q&amp;A: motor learning goes through three stages: <b>cognitive</b> (you have to think about it), <b>associative</b> (linking thinking and doing), and with time <b>autonomous</b> (it just happens), like walking.","w":["Wrong order.","Reversed.","Wrong order.","Not the stages described."],"k":"Motor learning: cognitive → associative → autonomous."},{"id":1106,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A cell is described as: found only in the CNS, non-excitable, about 5% of all brain cells, and able to promote repair. Which other feature would you also expect it to have?","o":["It is phagocytic","It myelinates multiple axons","It is star-shaped and forms the BBB","It releases neurotransmitter from its terminal","It has one axon and many dendrites"],"e":"These are features of <b>microglia</b>: ~5% of brain cells, promote repair, distributed in mosaic patterns, and <b>phagocytic/immune</b>.","w":["Oligodendrocyte.","Astrocyte.","Neuron.","Multipolar neuron."],"k":"Microglia: 5%, repair, phagocytic.","rn":[["","Microglia","Microglia","Resident immune cells of the brain; Phagocytic; About 5% of brain cells"]]},{"id":1107,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A drug selectively damages the cells that remove excess K⁺ and take up glutamate. Which other function would also be lost?","o":["Maintenance of the BBB","Myelination of CNS axons","Phagocytosis of pathogens","Regeneration of PNS axons","Formation of the nodes of Ranvier"],"e":"K⁺ removal and glutamate uptake are <b>astrocyte</b> functions. Astrocytes also form the <b>protective barrier around vessels: the BBB</b>, as well as structural support, glucose supply and repair.","w":["That is oligodendrocytes.","That is microglia.","That is Schwann cells.","Nodes are gaps between myelin segments."],"k":"Astrocytes: K⁺, glutamate, BBB, glucose, support, repair.","rn":[["","What are the 6 functions of Astrocytes?","What are the 6 functions of Astrocytes?","Structural support; Supply of nutrients; Maintain ionic environment; Neurotransmitter uptake; Repair; Protective barrier around vessels (BBB); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1108,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which statement is <b>false</b>?","o":["Schwann cells myelinate several axons each","Oligodendrocytes are found only in the CNS","Microglia are activated by pathogens","Astrocytes are star-shaped","Glia are non-excitable"],"e":"A single <b>Schwann cell myelinates a single axon</b>. It is the oligodendrocyte that myelinates multiple axons. All the other statements are true.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Schwann = one axon.","rn":[["","Schwann Cell","Schwann Cell","Neuroglia of the PNS; Myelinates a single axon; Spiral wrapping"],["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"]]},{"id":1109,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which of these structures contains the <b>least</b> myelin?","o":["The cerebral cortex","The corpus callosum","The white matter of the arbor vitae","A myelinated peripheral nerve","A spinal cord white-matter tract"],"e":"The <b>cerebral cortex</b> is the outer layer of <b>grey matter</b>, made mostly of cell bodies with little myelin. The corpus callosum, cerebellar white matter, myelinated nerves and white-matter tracts are all heavily myelinated.","w":["A white-matter tract.","White matter.","Myelinated.","White matter."],"k":"Cortex = grey matter (little myelin).","rn":[["","In a cut brain, where are white matter (WM) and grey matter (GM) found?","In a cut brain, where are white matter (WM) and grey matter (GM) found?","White matter: innermost; Grey matter: outer layer (cerebral cortex)"],["","Cerebral Cortex","Cerebral Cortex","Outer layer of grey matter of the cerebral hemispheres; 10-20 billion neurons; 2.5 mm thick"]]},{"id":1110,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which pairing of cell and Greek/Latin meaning is correct?","o":["Astrocyte – star","Cerebellum – tree of life","Glia – little brain","Oligodendrocyte – glue","Arbor vitae – little brain"],"e":"<b>Astro = star</b> (astrocytes' star shape). Glia = glue; oligodendrocyte = cell with few processes; cerebellum = little brain; arbor vitae = tree of life.","w":["Cerebellum = little brain.","Glia = glue.","Oligodendrocyte = cell with few processes.","Arbor vitae = tree of life."],"k":"Know the word meanings: astro = star, glia = glue, cerebellum = little brain.","rn":[["","Astrocyte","Astrocyte","Star-shaped glial cell; \"Remarkable cells\"; Forms the blood-brain barrier"],["","Why are astrocytes called astrocytes?","Why are astrocytes called astrocytes?","Star-shaped morphology; Astro = star"]]},{"id":1111,"t":"Divisions of the Nervous System","s":"A researcher counts nerves leaving the CNS: 12 pairs from the brain and brainstem and 31 pairs from the spinal cord. What is the total number of individual nerves (left and right counted separately)?","o":["86","43","62","24","74"],"e":"12 pairs of cranial nerves + 31 pairs of spinal nerves = 43 pairs = <b>86</b> individual nerves.","w":["That is the number of pairs.","That is only the spinal nerves (31 × 2).","That is only the cranial nerves (12 × 2).","Incorrect arithmetic."],"k":"12 cranial + 31 spinal = 43 pairs.","rn":[["","How many pairs of cranial nerves are there?","How many pairs of cranial nerves are there?","12 pairs"],["","How many pairs of spinal nerves are there?","How many pairs of spinal nerves are there?","31 pairs"]]},{"id":1112,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient has damage to the lobe responsible for formation of new memories, and also notices altered hearing. A second patient has a lesion causing loss of balance. Which structures are affected in the first and second patients respectively?","o":["Temporal lobe; cerebellum","Frontal lobe; brainstem","Parietal lobe; cerebellum","Temporal lobe; occipital lobe","Occipital lobe; hypothalamus"],"e":"Memory (hippocampus on the medial temporal lobe) and hearing (primary auditory cortex) → <b>temporal lobe</b>. Balance/posture → <b>cerebellum</b>.","w":["Frontal = motor/executive.","Parietal = somatosensory.","Occipital = vision.","Occipital = vision; hypothalamus = homeostasis."],"k":"Temporal: hearing + memory. Cerebellum: balance.","rn":[["","Hippocampus","Hippocampus","Medial aspect of the temporal lobe; Memory centre; Formation and consolidation of new memories"],["","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Temporal Lobe?","Primary auditory cortex; Primary olfactory (smell) cortex; Learning and memory"],["","How do you remember the lobe functions?","How do you remember the lobe functions?","Frontal = Function (executive) + movement; Parietal = Pain / touch; Temporal = Tunes (hearing) + memory; Occipital = Optics (vision)"]]},{"id":1113,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"After a stroke, a patient loses touch and proprioception on one side, with normal strength. Name the lobe labelled <b>B</b>, which contains the affected primary cortex.","e":"B is the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus; it contains the primary somatosensory cortex (pain, touch, proprioception).","k":"B = parietal.","img":{"src":"1/lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Labels removed."},"a":["parietal","parietal lobe"],"rn":[["","What are the functions of the Parietal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Parietal Lobe?","Primary somatosensory cortex; Pain, touch and proprioception"]]},{"id":1114,"t":"Anatomical Terminology","s":"In the body, the term “dorsal” means the back. A student applies this to a brain diagram and labels the back of the brain as dorsal. Which term should they have used, and what does dorsal mean in the brain?","o":["Caudal; the top","Rostral; the underside","Ventral; the top","Caudal; the underside","Lateral; the front"],"e":"In the brain the back is <b>caudal</b>, and <b>dorsal = top</b>.","w":["Rostral = front; dorsal is not the underside.","Ventral = underside.","Dorsal is the top.","Lateral = away from the midline."],"k":"Back of the brain = caudal; dorsal = top.","rn":[["","In the brain, what does caudal mean?","In the brain, what does caudal mean?","Back"],["","In the brain, what does dorsal mean?","In the brain, what does dorsal mean?","Up (top)"]]},{"id":1115,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"Which pairing of cell and location is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Oligodendrocyte – peripheral nerve","Schwann cell – peripheral nerve","Pyramidal neuron – hippocampus","Unipolar sensory neuron – dorsal root ganglion","Bipolar neuron – retina"],"e":"Oligodendrocytes are found <b>only in the CNS</b>. Peripheral nerves are myelinated by Schwann cells. The other pairings are correct.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Oligodendrocytes: CNS only.","rn":[["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"]]},{"id":1116,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which statement about glial cell numbers is most accurate?","o":["Glia outnumber neurons about 10:1, though this is questioned","Neurons outnumber glia about 10:1, though this is questioned","Glia and neurons are present in roughly equal numbers","Glia outnumber neurons about 10:1, and this is firmly established","Glia make up about 5% of all brain cells"],"e":"Glial cells are the <b>most numerous</b> cells in the CNS, outnumbering neurons by about <b>10:1</b>, although <b>some evidence questions this</b>. Don't worry about exact statistics; the principle matters.","w":["Glia are the most numerous cells.","Not the lecture figure.","Some evidence questions the ratio.","5% is microglia only."],"k":"Glia ~10:1 to neurons (debated).","rn":[["","What is the ratio of glial cells to neurons?","What is the ratio of glial cells to neurons?","About 10:1; Some evidence questions this"]]},{"id":1117,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the reticular theory is correct?","o":["It proposed one continuous network and is now obsolete","It proposed discrete cells and is still accepted","It proposed one continuous network and is still accepted","It was proposed by Cajal and replaced the neuronal doctrine","It proposed discrete cells and was replaced by Cajal"],"e":"The <b>reticular theory</b> held that everything in the nervous system is a <b>single continuous network</b>. It was formerly prominent but is now <b>obsolete</b>, replaced by Cajal's neuronal doctrine.","w":["Discrete cells is the neuronal doctrine.","It is obsolete.","Cajal gave the neuronal doctrine, which replaced it.","It proposed a continuous network."],"k":"Reticular theory: continuous network, obsolete.","rn":[["","Reticular Theory","Reticular Theory","Everything in the nervous system is a single continuous network; Formerly prominent; Now obsolete"]]},{"id":1118,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A student describes a primary sensory neuron in the dorsal root ganglion. Which combination is correct?","o":["Unipolar; cell body in a ganglion in the PNS","Bipolar; cell body in a ganglion in the PNS","Unipolar; cell body in the dorsal horn of the cord","Multipolar; cell body in a ganglion in the PNS","Multipolar; cell body in the dorsal horn of the cord"],"e":"Primary sensory neurons are <b>unipolar</b> with cell bodies in the <b>dorsal root ganglia</b>. A ganglion is a cluster of neuronal cell bodies.","w":["Bipolar neurons were the retina example.","The cell body is in the dorsal root ganglion (PNS).","Primary sensory neurons are unipolar.","Wrong morphology and location."],"k":"DRG primary sensory neurons are unipolar.","rn":[["","Which neuron type is the ganglion cell of the dorsal root?","Which neuron type is the ganglion cell of the dorsal root?","Pseudo-unipolar"],["","Example of a unipolar neuron","Example of a unipolar neuron","Primary sensory neurons; e.g. cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia"]]},{"id":1119,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"Which of the following correctly describes the direction of information flow in the dendrite and the axon?","o":["Dendrite toward the soma; axon away from the soma","Dendrite away from the soma; axon toward the soma","Both toward the soma","Both away from the soma","Dendrite toward the axon terminal; axon toward the dendrite"],"e":"The <b>dendrite</b> detects incoming information and conducts <b>toward the cell body</b>; the <b>axon</b> conducts <b>away from the soma</b> toward the axon terminal.","w":["Reversed.","The axon conducts away.","The dendrite conducts toward.","Not correct."],"k":"Dendrite → soma; soma → axon → terminal.","rn":[["","Axon","Axon","Conducts impulses away from the soma; Toward the axon terminal; May be myelinated"],["","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","What are the 4 component parts of a neuron?","Dendrite; Soma (cell body); Axon; Axon terminal; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1120,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the hypothalamus and brainstem is correct?","o":["Hypothalamus is forebrain; brainstem is midbrain + hindbrain","Hypothalamus is midbrain; brainstem is midbrain + hindbrain","Hypothalamus is forebrain; brainstem is hindbrain only","Hypothalamus is part of the brainstem with the medulla","Hypothalamus is forebrain; brainstem is forebrain + midbrain"],"e":"The <b>hypothalamus</b> is in the <b>forebrain</b>, below (ventral to) the thalamus. The <b>brainstem</b> is formed by the <b>midbrain and hindbrain</b>.","w":["The hypothalamus is forebrain.","The brainstem includes the midbrain.","It is forebrain, below the thalamus.","The brainstem is midbrain and hindbrain."],"k":"Hypothalamus: forebrain, below thalamus. Brainstem: midbrain + hindbrain.","rn":[["","What is the midbrain?","What is the midbrain?","The interface between the forebrain and the hindbrain; Part of the brainstem"],["","What forms the brainstem?","What forms the brainstem?","Midbrain; Hindbrain"],["","What are the 3 divisions of the brain?","What are the 3 divisions of the brain?","Forebrain; Midbrain; Hindbrain"]]},{"id":1121,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A 30-year-old woman has a condition in which the myelin of her brain and spinal cord is damaged, while her peripheral nerves are spared. A biopsy of the affected white matter shows that one type of glial cell is being lost. Researchers note that losing just one of these cells removes the myelin from several nearby axons at once.<br><br>Which cell is being lost, and why does the loss of one cell affect several axons?","o":["Oligodendrocytes, because each one myelinates multiple axons in the CNS","Schwann cells, because each one myelinates multiple axons in the CNS","Schwann cells, because each one wraps a single axon by spiral wrapping in the periphery","Microglia, because they make myelin basic protein for many axons","Astrocytes, because they form the myelin sheath around brain vessels and axons"],"e":"Step 1: the damage is in the brain and spinal cord (CNS) with the peripheral nerves spared, and the CNS myelinating cell is the oligodendrocyte (Schwann cells are the PNS equivalent). Step 2: each oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at the same time, whereas a Schwann cell myelinates a single axon, so the loss of one oligodendrocyte strips myelin from several axons.","w":["Schwann cells are found in the PNS, not the CNS.","Schwann cells do wrap one axon each, but they are PNS cells and the PNS is spared here.","Microglia are phagocytic immune cells, not myelinating cells.","Astrocytes support neurons and form the blood–brain barrier; they do not make myelin."],"k":"Oligodendrocyte = CNS, many axons; Schwann cell = PNS, one axon.","rn":[["","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell vs oligodendrocyte: what is the key difference in myelination?","Schwann cell myelinates a single axon (PNS, peripheral nervous system); Oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons at once (CNS, central nervous system)"],["","Why does damage to oligodendrocytes affect many neurons at once?","Why does damage to oligodendrocytes affect many neurons at once?","One cell myelinates axons of multiple neurons; So impaired oligodendrocytes impair many neurons"],["","Oligodendrocyte","Oligodendrocyte","CNS glial cell; Produces and maintains the myelin sheath; Each cell myelinates multiple axons"]]},{"id":1122,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A 25-year-old man cuts a nerve in his forearm with a kitchen knife. Over the following months, the injured axons slowly regrow along their original path and some feeling returns. In contrast, a patient with a similar cut in the spinal cord shows little regrowth. A neurologist explains that a glial cell found only at the forearm site makes the difference.<br><br>Which cell is responsible, and what does it do to allow regrowth?","o":["Schwann cells, which provide a substrate for the axon to grow along","Oligodendrocytes, which divide to replace the lost axons in the forearm","Microglia, which produce new neurons in the neurogenic niches","Astrocytes, which form glial scars that guide the axon all the way back to its original target muscle","Neurons, which divide by mitosis to replace the damaged cells"],"e":"Step 1: the forearm nerve is part of the PNS, whose glial cell is the Schwann cell. Step 2: Schwann cells are important for regeneration because they provide a substrate for the axon to grow along; this role is not highlighted for oligodendrocytes in the CNS.","w":["Oligodendrocytes are found only in the CNS and are not the regeneration cell here.","Microglia are immune cells; neurogenic niches are areas of limited adult neurogenesis in the brain.","Astrocytes form glial scars as part of CNS repair; they are not in the forearm nerve.","Neurons are post-mitotic and cannot divide."],"k":"Schwann cells support PNS axon regeneration as a growth substrate.","rn":[["","Why are Schwann cells important for regeneration?","Why are Schwann cells important for regeneration?","Provide a substrate for the axon to grow along"]]},{"id":1123,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A 78-year-old man has a progressive dementia. Brain imaging over five years shows steady atrophy (shrinkage) of his cortex. His daughter asks why the lost brain cells are not simply replaced, as skin cells are after a cut. The doctor mentions that there is limited evidence of new neurons forming in a few specific areas of the adult brain.<br><br>Which statement best explains the atrophy?","o":["Neurons are post-mitotic, so lost neurons are not replaced except in neurogenic niches","Neurons divide quickly, but glial cells consume the new neurons","Glial cells are non-excitable, so they cannot support the remaining neurons","Neurons are replaced by Schwann cells, which cannot carry electrical impulses within the brain cortex","Chemical synapses are slower than electrical synapses, so neurons die"],"e":"Step 1: neurons are post-mitotic cells, meaning they cannot proliferate (divide). Step 2: neurons that are lost are therefore not replaced, which is why the cortex shrinks in dementia; the only exception is limited adult neurogenesis in specific \"neurogenic niches\".","w":["Neurons do not divide; they are post-mitotic.","Glia being non-excitable does not mean they fail to support neurons; they are the support cells.","Schwann cells are PNS myelinating cells and do not replace brain neurons.","Synapse speed does not explain why lost neurons are not replaced."],"k":"Post-mitotic neurons cannot divide, so neuron loss is permanent.","rn":[["","Post-mitotic","Post-mitotic","Cannot proliferate (divide)"],["","Neurogenic Niches","Neurogenic Niches","Specific areas of the brain with some adult neurogenesis"],["","Can astrocytes regenerate neurons?","Can astrocytes regenerate neurons?","No (neurons are post-mitotic); But astrocytes can initiate a process of repair"]]},{"id":1124,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"In a laboratory model, the astrocytes in a region of brain tissue are selectively destroyed. Soon afterwards, the fluid around the neurons shows a rise in potassium (K⁺) and in the neurotransmitter glutamate. The neurons are also short of glucose, and the small blood vessels become leaky.<br><br>Which set of astrocyte functions has been lost?","o":["K⁺ removal, glutamate uptake, glucose supply and the blood–brain barrier","Myelin production, phagocytosis, glucose supply and K⁺ removal","Myelination of multiple axons and production of myelin basic protein","Phagocytosis of pathogens and transformation to the activated state","Electrical conduction of impulses from the soma toward the axon terminal"],"e":"Step 1: the findings are raised K⁺, raised glutamate, low glucose for neurons and leaky vessels. Step 2: astrocytes maintain the ionic environment (removal of excess K⁺), take up neurotransmitter (glutamate), supply nutrients (glucose) and form a protective barrier around vessels (the blood–brain barrier), so all four functions have been lost.","w":["Myelin production is done by oligodendrocytes and phagocytosis by microglia.","Myelination of multiple axons is the job of oligodendrocytes.","Phagocytosis and activation describe microglia.","Glial cells are non-excitable and do not conduct impulses."],"k":"Astrocytes: support, glucose, K⁺ removal, glutamate uptake, repair, BBB.","rn":[["","What are the 6 functions of Astrocytes?","What are the 6 functions of Astrocytes?","Structural support; Supply of nutrients; Maintain ionic environment; Neurotransmitter uptake; Repair; Protective barrier around vessels (BBB); MNEMONIC"],["","BBB","BBB","Blood Brain Barrier"]]},{"id":1125,"t":"Neuroglia​","s":"A 40-year-old patient develops a brain infection. A brain biopsy shows that a type of cell, normally delicately branched and spread in a regular mosaic pattern, has changed shape and is now engulfing debris and pathogens. These cells normally make up only about 5% of the cells in the brain.<br><br>Which cell is this, and what change has occurred?","o":["Microglia that have moved from a quiescent to an activated state","Astrocytes that have stopped supplying glucose and are now forming the blood–brain barrier","Oligodendrocytes that have stopped making myelin and started dividing","Schwann cells that have entered the brain to support regeneration","Neurons that have become phagocytic after losing their dendrites"],"e":"Step 1: delicately ramified (branched) cells in a regular mosaic pattern, making up about 5% of brain cells, are microglia. Step 2: microglia are activated by pathogens and transform from a quiescent (resting) to an activated state, acting as phagocytic and immune cells that also promote repair.","w":["Astrocytes support neurons and form the BBB; they are not the phagocytic 5% population.","Oligodendrocytes make myelin and are not described as phagocytic.","Schwann cells are PNS cells.","Neurons are excitable cells, not phagocytes."],"k":"Microglia: ramified, ~5%, activated by pathogens, phagocytic.","rn":[["","What happens to microglia after they phagocytose a pathogen?","What happens to microglia after they phagocytose a pathogen?","Return to the quiescent state"],["","What 2 states can microglia be in?","What 2 states can microglia be in?","Quiescent (resting); Activated"],["","What activates microglia?","What activates microglia?","Pathogens"]]},{"id":1126,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A 62-year-old right-handed man has a small stroke. He can no longer feel light touch or pain on part of his body and cannot tell where his limb is in space without looking, but his muscle strength is normal. His hearing, vision and sense of smell are unaffected.<br><br>Which lobe is most likely damaged, and which landmark lies just in front of it?","o":["Parietal lobe; the central sulcus separates it from the frontal lobe","Frontal lobe; the central sulcus separates it from the occipital lobe","Temporal lobe; it contains the primary somatosensory cortex","Occipital lobe; it is separated from the frontal lobe by the central sulcus","Cerebellum, which lies under the arbor vitae and controls touch"],"e":"Step 1: loss of pain, touch and proprioception with normal strength points to the primary somatosensory cortex, which is in the parietal lobe. Step 2: the central sulcus separates the frontal lobe (motor, in front) from the parietal lobe (somatosensory, behind), so the motor cortex in front is spared.","w":["The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex and strength is normal here.","The temporal lobe holds auditory and olfactory cortex and memory.","The occipital lobe is the primary visual cortex and vision is normal.","The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance; it is not the somatosensory cortex."],"k":"Parietal = somatosensory, behind the central sulcus.","rn":[["","What does the central sulcus separate?","What does the central sulcus separate?","Frontal lobe (motor, in front); Parietal lobe (somatosensory, behind)"]]},{"id":1127,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A 50-year-old motorcyclist suffers a severe head injury. CT shows bleeding that compresses the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata. Within minutes his breathing becomes irregular and his blood pressure and heart rate become unstable. The team warns his family that the outlook is very poor.<br><br>Which statement best explains his deterioration?","o":["The brainstem controls respiration and the cardiovascular system, so damage is likely fatal","The cerebellum controls breathing, so its compression stops respiration","The hypothalamus lies in the medulla and controls the heart","The occipital lobe controls respiration through the primary visual cortex","The spinal cord ends at C1, so the injury has cut it off from the brain and stopped all motor output"],"e":"Step 1: the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata together form the brainstem (the midbrain and hindbrain). Step 2: the brainstem connects the cortex to the spinal cord and controls respiration and the cardiovascular system, so damage here is most likely fatal.","w":["The cerebellum coordinates and modifies muscular activity and balance.","The hypothalamus is part of the forebrain, ventral to the thalamus.","The occipital lobe is the primary visual cortex.","The spinal cord extends from C1 to L1; it does not end at C1."],"k":"Brainstem controls breathing and the CVS; damage is likely fatal.","rn":[["","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Why is the brainstem functionally critical?","Connects the cortex to the spinal cord; Controls respiration and the cardiovascular system"]]},{"id":1128,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A 35-year-old woman develops a small tumour just below the thalamus. Over several months she feels constantly hungry and never full, her body temperature swings, and she develops hormone problems. She also has abnormal sweating and heart-rate control.<br><br>Which structure is affected, and which broad role explains all her problems?","o":["Hypothalamus; it controls homeostasis via the ANS, endocrine system, temperature and satiety","Cerebellum; it coordinates muscular activity and regulates sensory systems","Brainstem; it connects the cortex to the spinal cord","Frontal lobe; it controls executive functions such as thinking and considering","Temporal lobe; its primary olfactory cortex controls hunger, hormones and body temperature"],"e":"Step 1: a structure just below (ventral to) the thalamus is the hypothalamus. Step 2: the hypothalamus is important in homeostasis and contributes to control of the autonomic nervous system (sweating, heart rate), the endocrine system (hormones), thermoregulation (temperature) and satiety (suppression of hunger), matching all her problems.","w":["The cerebellum is in the hindbrain and handles coordination and balance.","The brainstem controls respiration and the CVS, not satiety or temperature.","The frontal lobe is not described as controlling satiety or hormones.","The temporal lobe handles hearing, smell and memory, not homeostasis."],"k":"Hypothalamus: ANS, endocrine, thermoregulation, satiety.","rn":[["","What does the hypothalamus contribute to controlling?","What does the hypothalamus contribute to controlling?","Autonomic nervous system; Endocrine system; Thermoregulation; Satiety; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":1129,"t":"Cells of the Nervous System","s":"A neuroscience student examines three neurons under a microscope. Neuron X has a single axon only and its cell body lies in a dorsal root ganglion. Neuron Y has one axon and one dendrite and is found next to the photoreceptors of the retina. Neuron Z, from the cerebellum, has one axon and many dendrites.<br><br>Which classification is correct?","o":["X unipolar; Y bipolar; Z multipolar (Purkinje)","X bipolar; Y unipolar; Z multipolar (pyramidal)","X multipolar; Y bipolar; Z unipolar","X unipolar; Y multipolar; Z bipolar","All three multipolar, as most brain neurons have many dendrites and a single long axon"],"e":"Step 1: the number of processes defines the type: unipolar = single axon only, bipolar = one axon and one dendrite, multipolar = one axon and multiple dendrites. Step 2: X (dorsal root ganglion sensory neuron) is unipolar, Y (retinal bipolar cell) is bipolar, and Z is a multipolar Purkinje neuron of the cerebellum (pyramidal neurons are in the hippocampus).","w":["X and Y are swapped, and pyramidal neurons are hippocampal.","X has only one process, so it cannot be multipolar.","Y has only two processes, so it cannot be multipolar.","Only Z has multiple dendrites."],"k":"Unipolar (DRG), bipolar (retina), multipolar (Purkinje, cerebellum).","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of neuron by shape?","What are the 3 types of neuron by shape?","Unipolar; Bipolar; Multipolar"]]},{"id":1130,"t":"Peripheral Nervous System","s":"A 45-year-old man has a peripheral nerve injury in his thigh. He loses voluntary control of a skeletal muscle, loses skin sensation, and the skin over the area stops sweating normally. The nerve arises from the lumbar part of the spinal cord. His doctor notes that peripheral nerves are often not anatomically separated into components.<br><br>Which statement best explains why all three functions are lost?","o":["The nerve carries both somatic and autonomic axons, as many peripheral nerves do","The nerve is a cranial nerve, which carries only autonomic axons","The nerve is part of the CNS, so it contains oligodendrocytes","Somatic axons control both the skeletal muscles and sweating glands of the skin","The lumbar region has 12 spinal nerve pairs, so damage spreads widely"],"e":"Step 1: loss of voluntary muscle control is a somatic (voluntary) function, while sweating is an autonomic (involuntary) response; peripheral nerves convey motor, sensory and autonomic axons. Step 2: many peripheral nerves contain axons from both the somatic and autonomic components, so they are not always anatomically separate, and one injury can knock out all three.","w":["A nerve arising from the lumbar cord is a spinal nerve, not a cranial nerve.","Peripheral nerves are part of the PNS, myelinated by Schwann cells.","Sweating is an autonomic response; somatic axons control skeletal muscles.","There are 5 lumbar pairs; 12 is the thoracic number."],"k":"Peripheral nerves are often mixed: somatic + autonomic axons.","rn":[["","Are the somatic and autonomic components always anatomically separate?","Are the somatic and autonomic components always anatomically separate?","No; Many peripheral nerves contain axons from both"],["","What types of axons do peripheral nerves carry?","What types of axons do peripheral nerves carry?","Motor; Sensory; Autonomic"]]},{"id":1501,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"After a stroke a patient loses vision, although the eyes are normal. Name the lobe labelled <b>D</b>, the likely site.","a":["occipital","occipital lobe"],"e":"D is the occipital lobe, containing the primary visual cortex. A frontal, B parietal, C temporal, E cerebellum.","k":"D = occipital.","img":{"src":"1/o_lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Image: Henry Vandyke Carter, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 lobes of the brain?","What are the 4 lobes of the brain?","Frontal; Parietal; Temporal; Occipital"],["","What is the function of the Occipital Lobe?","What is the function of the Occipital Lobe?","Primary visual cortex"]]},{"id":1502,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A patient sways and walks unsteadily with normal strength. Name the structure labelled <b>E</b>.","a":["cerebellum"],"e":"E is the cerebellum (“little brain”): coordination and balance; its white matter forms the arbor vitae.","k":"E = cerebellum.","img":{"src":"1/o_lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Image: Henry Vandyke Carter, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Cerebellum","Cerebellum","Latin for \"little brain\"; Coordination and modification of muscular activity"],["","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","What muscular activity does the cerebellum coordinate?","Motor movements; Regulating sensory systems; Balance / posture"]]},{"id":1503,"t":"Central Nervous System","s":"A tumour in region <b>A</b> changes a patient’s planning and decision-making. Which other function is located in this lobe?","o":["Primary motor cortex","Primary visual cortex","Primary auditory cortex","Primary somatosensory cortex","Formation of new memories"],"w":["Vision is in the occipital lobe (D).","Hearing and smell are in the temporal lobe (C).","Touch and proprioception are in the parietal lobe (B).","New memories depend on the hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe."],"e":"A is the frontal lobe: primary motor cortex, executive functions, thinking and planning.","k":"Frontal = motor + executive.","img":{"src":"1/o_lobes","cap":"Lateral view of the left cerebral hemisphere. Image: Henry Vandyke Carter, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What are the functions of the Frontal Lobe?","What are the functions of the Frontal Lobe?","Primary motor cortex; Executive functions; Ability to think and consider"]]}]);
